window.SPECS_BODY = {"booping:rule/0001":"Rule A lesson the user takes from a retro or a review is written as a rule: a record in specs/rule/ of its tier (project, user or core) with targets: — the playbooks, steps, agents or skills it applies to — and brief:, one or two lines. Only the brief is injected into a targeted step, with a link to the record. The brief is enough for most of the work on its own: an agent reads the record only for an edge case or the reason, never for every rule; and the brief does not restate what the agent checks or knows anyway. ## Follows from links a value, or the source the lesson came from (the retro, the review, the incident) when no value names it. Follows from Curated self-learning — a lesson becomes a rule only when the user takes it, and a rule is where taken knowledge lives. Minimal effective context — a step gets the brief, not the whole record. Single source — one record carries the rule and its reason; no second lesson file restates it.","booping:rule/0004":"Rule Grooming looks for what could stop the run — unclear scope, an undecided choice, a missing access — and settles it with the user in the grooming session before the plan reaches ready-for-dev. Develop carries the plan to the end on its own and stops only for force majeure: what grooming could not have known. Follows from Plans run to the end — a plan runs to the end only when its questions are answered before it starts. Effective human review — the user answers in one planning session, not in ad-hoc interruptions.","booping:rule/0006":"Rule Before a prompt-bearing artefact (skill, agent, partial, template, briefing) is saved, every block in it passes four checks: Scoping — the component needs this to do its job; what belongs to a higher-level orchestrator moves up, and a residue of a past correction with no positive rule attached is deleted. Duplication — the block lives nowhere else; a block shared by several components is extracted to a partial. Configurability — a behaviour the user may want to tune sits in a partial or in config, not hard-coded in prose. Hierarchy — top-level docs say what and when, deeper docs say how; a block whose detail does not match its level moves a layer up or down, and route-specific detail is linked from docs/ rather than inlined. Motivation, restated context and preambles are cut: prose decays, the schema does not. Follows from Minimal effective context — a body carries only what its step needs. Single source — a duplicated block drifts. In the retro on skill refactors, chat and develop one pass caught an inlined roster, its verbatim copy in a sibling skill, a negative rule without a positive one and a hard-coded threshold.","booping:rule/0007":"Rule When a plan's work invalidates references — a deleted partial, a renamed agent, a status line in CLAUDE.md, a broken doc link — cleaning them up is a milestone task inside the same sprint, with its own DoD. A half-stale working tree at sprint close is itself the documentation gap; a \"sweep it up after\" follow-up leaves the repo inconsistent and gets dropped. Follows from Single source — a stale reference is a second, wrong copy of a fact. In the retro on migrating the retro skill to the template pipeline, deleting a partial and updating a CLAUDE.md status block as their own milestone landed both inside the sprint.","booping:rule/0008":"Rule When a plan produces user-facing content (release notes, user docs, changelogs), the content includes only what its audience interacts with; internal churn — CLI internals, build plumbing, refactors the audience never touches — is left out. Audience scoping is decided at generation time and written into the task's DoD, not cleaned up in a post-edit. Follows from Minimal effective context — a reader gets only what they touch. In the retro on CLI logging and agent namespacing, generated release notes listed internal booping CLI changes users never invoke; the same oversharing recurred in a docs review.","booping:rule/0009":"Rule Every agent briefing states the exact shape the agent returns, limited to what the orchestrator needs — for example \"the final reply contains only the list of changed files\". Groom bounds the shape while folding DoD and Verify into the tasks, so develop's briefings inherit it. An unbounded contract lets the agent return surplus prose into the orchestrator's context. Follows from Minimal effective context — the orchestrator's context holds only what it acts on. In the retro on CLI logging and agent namespacing, mid-sprint the user had to tell the agent to return only the changed files.","booping:rule/0010":"Rule When a plan defines a user-facing output or a log format, its exact content — the fields of a line, the truncation rules, the trailers — is agreed with the user during grooming, not only the format described in prose. This is one case of rule/0004: an output contract settled in develop is iterated live instead of decided up front. Follows from Plans run to the end — develop does not stop to settle what grooming should have asked. _lessons/0010_lock-output-content-during-grooming.md (removed by migration 004) — the source names no retro.","booping:rule/0030":"Rule When grooming a plan, name the values that drive its main choices, and flag every place the plan departs from a value or a rule, with the reason. The user sees what works and where a value pulls the wrong way, and can refine the value — which raises the quality of later work. Follows from Adjustable control — the human sees the values and boundaries at work and can take control of them. Effective human review — the review reads the values in play instead of reverse-engineering them from the plan.","booping:rule/0031":"Rule When the user corrects you — rejects a proposal, rewrites an output, says it is wrong, answers a gate other than you suggested — run booping create core.corrections.template {vault}/corrections --set name={step} --set step={playbook}/{step} --set did=… --set said='{the user's words verbatim}' --set artifact={run artefact, vault-relative}. Add --set why=… when the user states the reason. A plain confirmation is not a correction. One file per correction under {vault}/corrections/, its frontmatter stamped with the session id and the time; fields: core.corrections.template. Follows from Curated self-learning — a correction is logged, not turned into a rule; it reaches a rule only through retro and learn with the user. Analysable history — the log is plain markdown in the vault, with the session it came from. Corrections log — the decision to log corrections as they happen instead of mining transcripts at retro. Journal entries are vault files — one file per correction, written by booping create.","booping:rule/0032":"Rule At a review gate, the agent presents the artefact and asks for approval in prose, at the end of the message. It never asks through a multiple-choice question tool: approval means the user may read and answer with feedback of any length, which a set of options does not allow, and a prose question distracts less (owner, 2026-10-16). A reply that asks for changes sends the run back to the step that drafts the artefact; the gate does not absorb the change and ask again. The same holds for every decision the user has to understand the context to make — settling a conflict, a design fork, a review finding, a departure from a record: the agent asks in prose, one decision at a time, each with the compact context it needs (what the records say, what the code does, where it came from) and its own proposal, and with a counter ([n/total]) when several are queued. The user reads, asks back, then decides; options the agent prepared cut that short (owner, 2026-10-17). What the user accepts at a gate is the decision list of booping:rule/0033. A multiple-choice tool is only for a mechanical pick whose answer needs no context, such as a branch name or which of two files. The driving protocol (src/templates/_partials/_playbook_driving.j2) carries this rule for every playbook, so it needs no targets. Follows from Effective human review — the review step takes the user's full feedback where it is convenient for them. Adjustable control — the user steers by writing back, not by picking from options the agent prepared.","booping:rule/0033":"Rule Before the user accepts anything — a gate, a plan, a record drafted from the dialogue, a package of answers — the agent shows every decision it will take as settled as a numbered list, one plain line each, readable at a glance, never only inside prose or folded into one sentence. Each line names its source: the user's words, or the agent's own proposal. The user accepts the list or clarifies lines of it. A decision that was never on such a list, or a question the user left unanswered, is not accepted: it stays open, and where it lands in a record or a plan it is marked [clarify] (owner, 2026-10-17). The driving protocol (src/templates/_partials/_playbook_driving.j2) and the specs guidance carry this rule for every playbook and every record, so it needs no targets. Follows from Effective human review — a list is read and answered line by line; a decision buried in prose is easy to miss. Adjustable control — the user sees what the agent decides alone and can stop it. Guides to the goal — the system is clear to the user: what it is about to take as decided is in plain sight. Postmortem: the budget gate slipped into the core unasked — a one-line proposal and an unanswered question became core code.","booping:rule/0034":"Rule A review or an audit of the specs puts every record to the tests in the format guide: an ADR needs a real choice with analysed options, a rule must change how the system behaves, nothing restates what config, code or a value already says. Each record that fails is listed as one line — the record, the type it fits or deletion, one reason — for the user to accept per booping:rule/0033; nothing is moved or deleted before that (owner, 2026-10-18). Follows from Single source — a record repeating config, code or a value is a second place to drift. Specs live with the work — a review keeps the set to what earns its place, so it is read and applied.","booping:rule/0035":"Rule Before launching grooms, sort the batch's items by class: the kind of mechanism each will need — a log, a register, a launcher, a report, a mode. Items of one class are groomed together in one plan, or one after another with the earlier plan linked in the next groom's brief. They are never groomed in parallel. Items of different classes keep their own grooms. Follows from Quality at a rational cost — two items of one class groomed together cost one design and one review, not two. Prose or code probes — grooming two same-shape items together gave one mechanism built from existing primitives; one after another gave one mechanism anchored on the first plan's; each groomed alone gave its own.","booping:rule/0036":"Rule An ADR is never accepted silently. The agent drafts it status: proposed and shows the owner a brief in the specs guide's locked shape: the chosen and rejected options with the reason, the chosen decision's key points, what the decision touches or reverses, what the agent decided alone, and the alternatives score. It ends with an objection ask, not a request for a yes: no objection lets the run go on. The answer sets the record's review level with its review_evidence — shown when the owner raised nothing, accepted only with the owner's words quoted, discussed when the owner engaged and agreed — and an objection sends the ADR back to the draft. An autonomous run logs the brief as an agent decision instead and accepts the ADR with no review key, so what the agent settled alone stays visible (owner, 2026-10-19). Follows from Effective human review — the brief puts the alternatives, the key points and any reversal where the owner reads them, instead of inside the record. Adjustable control — the owner can stop a decision without having to approve every one; the level records how far they engaged.","booping:rule/0037":"Rule An open item is filed only for what must outlive the session: work for later — a feature, a bug, a fix, a scope question — or a question for the owner that waits for an answer. Four kinds of ask are never items. An instruction for this session (\"ping me\", \"wait\", \"do it now\") and an answer to a gate or a UAT confirmation are done by the agent or kept in the session. A standing policy becomes a rule through the specs playbook. A decision becomes an ADR. Follows from Minimal effective context — the open items stay a short index of real work; an audit of another booping project: 11 of 34 open items were never work (private note, not published). Single source — a policy lives in a rule and a decision in an ADR, not duplicated into an item.","booping:rule/0038":"Rule Every id the agent says in chat — an open item, an ADR, a rule, a plan, any other record — is followed by its short title, so the user reads what it names without opening it. Newly filed items are listed one per line, in the user's language: Создан {id}: {title} in Russian, Created {id}: {title} in English. The driving protocol (src/templates/_partials/_playbook_driving.j2) carries this rule for every playbook, so it needs no targets. Follows from Effective human review — an id with its title is readable at a glance. Guides to the goal — the user follows the run without looking ids up.","booping:rule/0039":"Rule An experiment, eval or benchmark matrix runs in three stages. First a cheap screen on a small sample drops the arms that clearly lose. Then a shortlist runs the surviving arms on more cases. Significance is measured only for the finalists, and only when the decision needs it. Before each stage the agent names its size — the number of calls and a rough cost — and what the stage decides. A stage larger than a small screen is not launched without a stated reason. The driving protocol (src/templates/_partials/_playbook_driving.j2) carries this rule for every playbook, and the session-start block (src/templates/hooks/session_start.md.j2) for every session in a booping project, so it needs no targets. Follows from Quality at a rational cost — budget goes only to arms that survive a screen; on 2026-10-19 a 280-call matrix was stopped after 104 calls, which had already shown the arms to drop. Proven to work — significance is earned on the finalists, where it decides something.","booping:rule/0040":"Rule The only command the scorer runs is its body render. It reads no file, runs no other command and consults no codebase, vault or web; it judges the draft ADR from the parts its prompt pastes, and an alternative, record or primitive they do not show does not exist. Its tools: Bash pattern exposes Bash without restricting the command, so this rule and the eval's tools assert are the guard. Follows from adr/0041 — the scorer is given exactly what it judges. Proven to work — a score drawn from a fixed input is reproducible, so the eval measures the scorer and not what it happened to find. Minimal effective context — the pasted parts are what the judgement needs.","booping:rule/0041":"Rule The project manager does not do heavy reads in its own context. Reviewing a worker's artefact (a mockup, a diff, a report file), reading a long file, and scanning many files or logs go to a subagent — booping:booping-researcher or a general-purpose reviewer — with a bounded return contract (e.g. a verdict in at most five lines). The manager keeps only that verdict. The manager's own reads are limited to short receipts, status outputs and small targeted lookups. Follows from Minimal effective context — the manager's context carries the whole session; an artefact read into it crowds out the state it exists to hold; in another booping project the manager read a whole ~1.5k-line, ~110 KB HTML mockup into its own context to check it before publishing, where a subagent check returning at most five lines would have done (private note, not published). Bounded agent return contract — the delegated read returns only what the manager needs.","claude-booping:adr/0001":"Context The typed-records specs system (values, rules, ADRs, conflicts, stories, ideas, references, runbooks) grew in one project and was copied into four: three game projects and an assistant project. The copies drifted — none of the five shared schemas match — and the cross-file checker exists in one copy only. Some decisions belong to no single project: an engineering practice such as fakes and DI over mocks applies to every project of one person. Booping already merges config, playbooks and lessons across three tiers (core → global → project) and ships migrations with a watermark in the project marker. The owner settled the shape in an alignment session on 2026-10-14. Criteria One source for the format; projects stop drifting apart. A person's cross-project rules live once, not in each project. Everything stays reachable from the Obsidian vault. A rule from another tier resolves to the same record on every machine. Decision The specs format is a core feature of booping, in three tiers: core — booping's repo: the format (README, schemas, later the checker and the setup and upgrade playbooks) and the shipped records in specs/, beside the core playbooks/ — the rules whose targets steer core playbooks for every user. Booping's records about its own development — values, ADRs, stories, references, ideas, runbooks and the rules that concern only booping — are booping's project tier, the type directories at booping's vault root (vault/{type}/), which is also the dogfood of the format. global — <home_dir>/_specs/, booping's existing global config level: one person's values and rules for every project. project — the project's vault itself: the type directories (value/, rule/, adr/ …) sit at the vault root beside plans/ and retrospectives/, with no specs/ directory in between, so Obsidian shows them directly (owner, 2026-10-15). A project that wants specs to change in the same commit as its code points the tier at a directory in its repo instead, and the vault links to it. A reference without a namespace points into its own tier. Another tier is named by a namespace — booping: for core, the user's id for the global tier (default $USER, confirmed at setup) — never by a path such as ~, so the same reference names the same person's record on every machine. An unresolved namespace (a collaborator, CI) is a warning, not an error. A project that departs from a rule of a tier above records it in its own ADR or conflict. Changes to the format reach projects as booping migrations. Lessons become rules with targets and brief (booping:rule/0001). Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Core feature of booping accepted medium medium Reuses booping's tiers, migrations and vault A separate specs repo rejected medium high A second tier system next to booping's Copies per project rejected low high What exists now; already drifted Core feature of booping Booping ships the format and merges the global and project tiers the way it merges config and playbooks. Pros: booping's tier merge, migrations and vault already exist; lessons and rules join into one record; one place to look in Obsidian. Cons: booping grows; a project that wants specs needs booping. Complexity: medium — a checker, setup and upgrade playbooks, lesson injection from rules. Reversibility: medium — the records are plain files and outlive the tooling. A separate specs repo A standalone repo with the guide, the schemas and the checker; projects vendor it and are asked periodically to look at its updates. Pros: usable without booping. Cons: a second tier and update mechanism next to booping's; lessons stay a separate thing from rules. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Copies per project Each project keeps its own copy of the format and its own checker. Pros: no shared dependency. Cons: the copies drift — they already have; a person's cross-project rules repeat in every project or live outside specs. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences vault/_lessons/ was converted into rule records and removed by migration 004. The existing project copies migrate after the feature ships, each with a commit before the migration for rollback. [clarify] An exported global tier that a project imports, so a namespace resolves without the home dir, is out of scope for now. 2026-10-15: the core tier moved out of booping's vault to specs/ at the repo root and holds only the shipped targeted rules; booping's project tier is the type directories at the vault root (vault/{type}/). With both on one directory, a booping-only rule could not keep targets without reaching every user — rule/0013 and rule/0015 got their targets back as project rules. Confirmation: just specs-check validates both tiers; a project set up by the setup playbook resolves a booping: and a global reference. 2026-10-15: the project tier became the vault root itself — type directories beside plans/, no specs/ in between; specs kept in a repo are the exception, linked into the vault. Specs that live in a notes repo do not change in the same commit as the code; open items and a specs sync catch the drift instead.","claude-booping:adr/0002":"Context Booping's procedures started as skills: /groom, /develop, /retro, /learn, /chat, /help, /install, /code-review. A skill has no run state, so an interrupted procedure cannot resume in another session. The groom skill was ported to a playbook and ran in parallel with it during beta; the other skills followed. At authoring time the stateless /code-review skill was kept because it took an arbitrary scope with no plan, which a playbook step could not handle then. Five runs grooming the same feature measured the cost of the playbook shape. The third playbook version against the second skill run: +61% API calls, +38% cache read, about 1.75× wall-clock, final context −7%, user wait −48%. The remaining gap is call count — transitions and gate round-trips — which the note calls the state machine's floor. Decision /playbook is the only skill the plugin ships. Groom, develop, code-review, retro, learn, setup, migrate and playbook-authoring are core playbooks, discovered beside the user's global and project playbooks. A playbook carries run state in its own artifact and resumes across sessions; step bodies load one at a time. A new procedure is a playbook; a skill is written only for a surface /playbook cannot drive. Code review of an ad-hoc scope moved into the code-review playbook (plan: null), so the stateless skill went too. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict One playbook skill accepted high low Run state, resume, user-extensible procedures A skill per procedure rejected low high No run state; cheaper per run Playbooks beside the skills rejected medium high The beta shape; two copies of each procedure One playbook skill Every procedure is a playbook; /playbook lists, selects and drives them. Pros: run state persisted per artifact and resumable; the user adds procedures in their own tiers; one driving protocol instead of one per skill. Cons: more API calls and about 1.75× wall-clock against the skill on the measured groom runs. Complexity: high — a playbook engine, state machines, a driving protocol. Reversibility: low — every procedure and the user's own playbooks depend on it. A skill per procedure Each procedure is one skill body rendered at load. Pros: fewer calls; shorter wall-clock on the measured runs. Cons: no persisted run state, so no resume across sessions; every skill repeats its own driving logic. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Playbooks beside the skills The playbook ships next to the untouched skill, as during the groom, develop and retro ports. Pros: a fallback while the playbook is in beta. Cons: two copies of each procedure to keep in step. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences /chat and /help were retired; skills/ holds only playbook. A run costs more calls than the old skill did; further squeezing is batching transitions with work, which the metrics note calls diminishing returns. Authoring a procedure goes through the playbook-authoring playbook.","claude-booping:adr/0003":"Context Skill bodies were first hand-written SKILL.md files, then generated from src/templates/ into committed SKILL.md files. A build subcommand with src/files/ templates and a separate src/config_files.yaml produced the plugin-packaging files. The framework research found that config_files.yaml carried only effort: and could fold into normal config, \"killing the fourth config file and possibly the whole build step\", and classed the build as plugin packaging rather than framework. What a body needs — the merged config tiers, the vault, rules, the playbook set — belongs to one project and is known only when the skill is loaded in it. Decision One rendering stage, at runtime. skills/<name>/SKILL.md and agents/<name>.md are hand-authored thin shells: frontmatter (with effort: inlined) plus one booping render src/templates/… line that renders the body at load time with the full project context. Edits to src/templates/ are live; nothing in the repo is rebuilt. The build stage and config_files.yaml are gone. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Render at load accepted medium medium Project context available; edits live Generated files from a build rejected medium high A rebuild per edit; no per-project context Hand-written full bodies rejected low high Repeats shared fragments and config literals Render at load A thin shell calls the renderer when Claude Code loads the skill or agent. Pros: the body sees the merged config, vault and rules of the project it runs in; template edits take effect on the next load. Cons: every load runs the CLI; other costs are not named in the source. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. Generated files from a build A build step renders templates into committed files. Pros: the rendered body is visible in the repo. Cons: a rebuild after every edit; a fourth config file; the output cannot carry one project's config or rules. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Hand-written full bodies Each SKILL.md carries its whole body. Pros: no tooling. Cons: structured data from config is restated in prose and drifts; shared fragments repeat across skills. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences Each playbook commits its rendered report (_reports/output.md), and just snapshots diffs it against a fresh render. Graph and render problems surface as in-band STOP or Note notices at exit 0, never as crashes.","claude-booping:adr/0004":"Context Booping's structured data — query specs, template trees, macros, sprint scale, agents, task types — lives in config and is rendered from there. Users and projects need to add and override it: the global tier carries home_dir, the project tier carries agent registrations and settings such as the cross-review agent. Before v1.0.0 an unrecognised key produced an unknown-key warning. The framework research proposed a fourth tier between core and global — defaults shipped inside each playbook directory — and noted that a playbook could not ship config defaults of its own. Decision Three tiers, deep-merged core → global → project: src/config.yaml ← ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/booping/config.yaml ← {vault}/config.yaml. Later tiers win; lists replace wholesale. There is no schema gate and no tier restriction: any key loads in any tier. The top level is home_dir plus core; a key one playbook owns lives at core.{name}_playbook, a shared key directly under core, and a user's own playbook namespace copies that shape. Any mapping can be a query spec or a template tree, declared beside its consumer, never in a central registry. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Three tiers without a schema gate accepted low medium Any tier adds or overrides any key Schema gate with unknown-key warnings rejected medium high Dropped in v1.0.0; reason not named in the source A playbook-defaults tier rejected medium medium Proposed in research; not adopted Three tiers without a schema gate Core, global and project config deep-merge with no validation of key names. Pros: a user or project tier extends any part of the config, including a user's own playbook namespace, without registering it anywhere. Cons: a misspelt key is silently ignored; a consumer that needs a closed set, such as the tracker driver name, has to reject unknown values itself. Complexity: low. Reversibility: medium. Schema gate with unknown-key warnings The merge warns on a key the core schema does not declare. Pros: typos surface at load. Cons: a key booping does not know is not a typo by default — user code (playbooks, scripts, templates) reads its own keys, so a project's config extends booping's logic; a warning would flag every such key (owner, 2026-10-14). Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. A playbook-defaults tier Each playbook ships its own config defaults in its directory, merged between core and global. Pros: a playbook owns its keys and their defaults; core config shrinks to framework keys. Cons: a fourth tier to resolve and document; not named further in the source. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. Consequences Core playbooks' keys live in src/config.yaml under core.{name}_playbook. core.tracker.driver treats an unknown name as a hard error, because the merge has no schema gate to catch it. [clarify] Whether a playbook-defaults tier is rejected or only deferred is not named in the source.","claude-booping:adr/0006":"Context Plans had one lifecycle in core config (plan.statuses, superstates, post hooks) driven by a plan-only transition command, while playbooks drove their own machines through playbook-transition. When groom became a playbook, its machine was designed as its own subset of the plan lifecycle joined to develop's at the edges — \"two different subsets of one machine, not one shared monolithic machine\". Develop first wrote the shared lifecycle names straight onto status:. A later fix showed the cost of shared names: develop's entry status awaiting-plan-review matched no real plan, because in groom it is a superstate that is never written to a file. Decision The shared plan-lifecycle vocabulary is gone. Each playbook declares named machines in its playbook.yaml states: block; those blocks are the authoritative status sets. Playbooks hand off at a status the next one accepts as entry — groom ends at ready-for-dev, develop starts there or at awaiting-approval, where handing the plan to develop is the approval. booping playbook-transition is the only writer of run state and its printed mutation report is authoritative — never re-read to verify; booping playbook-state is the read-only frontier used to resume. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Per-playbook machines accepted medium low Each playbook owns and documents its statuses One lifecycle in core config rejected medium medium A second dispatcher; plan domain in core One monolithic machine shared by playbooks rejected high medium Rejected at the groom port Per-playbook machines Each playbook's states: block is its own machine over its own artifact. Pros: one dispatcher for every machine; a playbook's statuses are read in one file; user playbooks work the same way. Cons: hand-off statuses must agree between two files; the README's Statuses section is hand-maintained narrative over them. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: low. One lifecycle in core config Plan statuses and hooks stay in src/config.yaml with their own transition command. Pros: one place lists every plan status. Cons: two dispatchers with duplicated idempotency, hooks and reporting; plan hooks unreachable from playbook machines. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. One monolithic machine shared by playbooks Groom and develop drive one machine covering the whole plan lifecycle. Pros: the hand-off is inside one machine. Cons: not named in the source beyond its rejection at the groom port. Complexity: high. Reversibility: medium. Consequences A status a playbook reads from another must be one the other actually writes, not a superstate. Hooks run after status: is written, so a failing hook leaves the artifact advanced; hooks that talk to the outside absorb their failures (adr/0019).","claude-booping:adr/0007":"Context Playbooks gained opt-in states:. Authoring needed a way to decide whether a playbook gets a machine and how much of one. Confirmations had been confirmed: flags flipped in a file — a hand-rolled two-state machine enforced only by prose. Two machine roles appeared: a procedure tracker that lets a run resume, and an artifact lifecycle whose statuses are hand-off points other playbooks gate on. Criteria An interrupted run resumes where it stopped when a session boundary is likely. A confirmation that gates a hand-off is enforced mechanically. A short single-session run pays nothing for tracking. Decision Every playbook has a state machine; the authoring question is whether it is persisted, at one of three tiers: ephemeral (no states:; gates are the transitions and the conversation is the storage), minimal (the confirmation chain only) or rich (superstates, script hooks, per-instance machines, a hand-off contract). The tier is authored: decompose records a verdict with its reason, the states step acts on it, and the user can override it at that gate. The strongest reason for a rich machine is an artifact other playbooks gate on; against a machine are a short single-session run, conversational gates, and an artifact whose lifecycle already lives elsewhere. A confirmation that must persist is an awaiting-* status whose exit transition requires explicit user confirmation — silence never counts — and stamps reviewed_at on the reviewed file. Every active phase gets an explicit in-progress status. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Authored tier accepted medium medium Judgment per playbook, confirmed by the user Machine derived from the graph rejected medium medium A DAG is not a state machine Formal threshold rules rejected low high Over-mechanical for a judgment call Confirmed flags rejected low high Enforced only by prose Authored tier The author chooses ephemeral, minimal or rich from heuristics; the minimal tier is a stamp-out pattern in the template. Pros: machines exist only where resume or a hand-off pays for them; confirmations become harness-enforced. Cons: each transition is a call; a judgment the user has to review. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. Machine derived from the graph A wave-by-wave state machine generated from graph:. Pros: no authoring step. Cons: the graph orders work; it does not name hand-off statuses or gates. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. Formal threshold rules Numeric rules pick the tier (inner depth above N and the like); raised in a cross-review. Pros: reproducible. Cons: over-mechanical for a class of decision booping leaves to prose plus a user gate. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Confirmed flags A confirmed: key the user or the runner flips in the artifact. Pros: no machine. Cons: nothing enforces it; the flag carries no history of who confirmed what. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences Code-review was first authored ephemeral and later got a machine once its review became a persisted artifact with re-review. Migrate stays ephemeral: the recorded migration id advances per migration and is the only state, so a machine would duplicate a ledger the vault already owns. A reviewed subject that cannot carry frontmatter stamps <subject>_reviewed_at on the machine's artifact instead.","claude-booping:adr/0008":"Context Retro was first bound to the plan lifecycle: the plan moved through awaiting-retro → awaiting-learning, and the retro playbook port put the retrospective at plans/{slug}/retro.md with the plan as the run's main artifact. A retro run may cover several plans, so the artifact had to live in a \"primary\" plan's directory with siblings linking to it. Code review was first authored ephemeral: no state machine, no artifact, no plan-status move, a re-review being a new run. Decision Plans end at develop's done and are never moved again. Retro's artifact is a standalone retrospectives/{YYYYMMDDHHMM}_{kebab-title}.md, one per run however many plans it covers, with its own machine (awaiting-retro → awaiting-learning); code review's artifact is codereviews/{plan-dirname}/{ts}.md with in-agent-review → human-review → done. Both machines declare no artifact: and are addressed with --target. The tracks join through plan frontmatter, not status: retro: is null until covered, so {status: done, retro: null} is the retro queue; code_reviews: lists every review that closed on the plan — history, not a queue flag — so every done plan stays reviewable. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Separate tracks joined by frontmatter accepted medium medium Plans stay done; queues are queries Retro statuses on the plan rejected low medium Superseded on 2026-08-07 Retro file inside the plan directory rejected low high A multi-plan retro needs a primary plan Ephemeral code review rejected low high No record; re-review invisible Separate tracks joined by frontmatter Each track has its own artifact and machine; a back-link key on the plan records the join. Pros: one retrospective per run regardless of plan count; review history accumulates on the plan; the queues are frontmatter queries. Cons: the join keys are written by hook scripts and must be kept in step with the artifacts. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. Retro statuses on the plan The plan itself moves through the retro and learning statuses. Pros: one artifact. Cons: a multi-plan retro moves several plans for one run; the plan lifecycle grows another playbook's statuses. Complexity: low. Reversibility: medium. Retro file inside the plan directory plans/{slug}/retro.md, sibling plans linking to the primary plan's file. Pros: the retro sits beside what it reviews. Cons: a primary plan has to be chosen for a multi-plan run. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Ephemeral code review The review lives only in the conversation. Pros: nothing to persist. Cons: no record of findings or verdicts; a reviewed plan is indistinguishable from an unreviewed one. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences Migration 002_tracks_and_lessons relocated plans/{slug}/retro.md into retrospectives/. A plan dropped from a retro is stamped retro: skipped by _scripts/drop-plan. Ad-hoc code reviews of no plan land in codereviews/{target-slug}/{ts}.md with plan: null.","claude-booping:adr/0009":"Context Plans were flat files plans/{slug}.md. The groom playbook needed a run workdir and a machine artifact. It first used a workdir-local run.md under _runs/ whose edge hooks stamped the plan file, then a plan directory with two documents: plan.md (the executable plan) and index.md (the machine artifact and evolving run document). The skill-versus-playbook measurements found the dual artifact a root cause of the playbook's cost: content authored twice, 21 Edits, late singleton edits at 130–157k context. Making index.md the only document cut the edits from 21 to 2. Decision A plan is the directory plans/{slug}/, which is also the run workdir. Its index.md is both the plan document and the machine's artifact. Run sidecars sit beside it: request.md (the framing brief), draft.md (the one-file plan draft before the split, adr/0030; there is no research-web step), clarifications.md, and the milestone directories. The discovery shape is the core.plans.glob config key. Flat plan files are converted by the migration 001_vault_layout. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Directory with one index.md accepted medium low One document; sidecars beside it Flat plan file rejected low medium No home for run sidecars Directory with plan.md and index.md rejected medium high Content authored twice A separate runs workdir rejected medium high Run and plan split; hooks stamp across Directory with one index.md The plan document carries the run status; everything else the run writes is a sidecar. Pros: one file to author and to read; the run resumes from the plan itself; groom and develop continue on the same file. Cons: every plan consumer had to change from the flat glob; a migration for existing vaults. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: low. Flat plan file plans/{slug}.md. Pros: simplest layout. Cons: research, briefs and milestone files have nowhere to live beside it. Complexity: low. Reversibility: medium. Directory with plan.md and index.md The executable plan and the run document as two files. Pros: the plan stays free of run bookkeeping. Cons: measured: content authored twice and 21 Edits against 2. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. A separate runs workdir A run.md in a separate workdir; hooks stamp the plan file by path. Pros: the plan file stays untouched by the procedure. Cons: two locations per run; a subgraph had to dissolve to make it work. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences index.md's ## Milestones table and the plan's sp are generated from the milestone files, not hand-kept. Plans groomed before this layout need the migration before develop can read them.","claude-booping:adr/0010":"Context Milestones were sections of the plan body; develop briefed a worker from the plan. A stopped sprint had no per-milestone progress to resume from, and a rejected attempt left no durable record of what was wrong. The plan's milestone table and story-point total were kept by hand. Decision Each milestone is a directory milestones/M{nn}-{kebab}/ holding the milestone file named after it — frontmatter id, title, sp, status, plan, plus tasks, DoD, Verify and References. The file is the contract handed to a worker by path; index.md is context only. Milestone status: is develop's per-instance run state (pending → in-progress → done, blocked off in-progress), so a stopped sprint resumes at the first milestone that is not done. A rejected attempt writes feedback.md beside the file — the only place attempts are counted. index.md's milestone table and sp are generated from the files. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Milestone files accepted medium low Contract by path; per-milestone resume Milestones inside the plan body rejected low medium Worker gets a slice; no per-milestone state Milestone files One file per milestone, with sidecars in its directory. Pros: the worker reads only its contract; status and attempts survive the session; the table and SP total cannot drift from the files. Cons: plans groomed before the change have no milestone files and must be re-groomed or finished first. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: low. Milestones inside the plan body Milestones are sections of index.md; the runner cuts the relevant slice into the briefing. Pros: one file per plan. Cons: no per-milestone status to resume from; the hand-kept table and SP total can disagree with the sections. Complexity: low. Reversibility: medium. Consequences Every milestone edge runs the refresh-milestone-table hook. The core.plans.milestones.glob matches the milestone file inside each directory, never a sidecar beside it.","claude-booping:adr/0011":"Context The first groom playbook spawned one sub-agent per step through a step agent: key. The skill-versus-playbook measurements traced the playbook's cost mostly to call count and to content carried in context, and found researcher delegation net-cheaper: a skill run that skipped the researcher paid about 1.2M extra main-thread cache-read for inline reads. Some steps need isolation for a different reason: code review's review step is detached so the diff never enters the runner's context. Decision Three delegation levels, set by the single detached: step field: inline — no detached:; the runner performs the step. assisted — the runner owns the step and hands heavy reads to the agent named by core.research_agent, which returns a compressed summary; the runner records the agent's id and resumes the same agent on loopbacks instead of spawning a fresh one. detached — detached: <model>:<effort> runs a generic sub-agent, any other string a named one; parallel co-members of a wave must be detached. The agent: key is gone, and a leftover one stops the run. Groom runs its steps in the driving session: its graph is intake → draft-plan → present, research is a stage of draft-plan that delegates bulk reads to the researcher (adr/0030), and the only detached spawn is draft-plan's split_fallback_agent; the cross-review step is removed (adr/0035) (adr/0031). Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Three levels in one field accepted medium medium Delegate only where it pays A sub-agent per step rejected low high Groom's first shape; more calls and re-briefing Detached parallel briefs rejected medium high Hands vault reads to agents Three levels in one field The runner keeps the conversation and the vault; agents get bounded work. Pros: the runner's context stays small where reads are heavy; an assisted agent keeps its warm context across loopbacks; one field replaces agent:. Cons: an assisted step's body must brief its agent itself; a detached step's body goes to the agent, so the runner never reads it. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. A sub-agent per step Every step runs in its own sub-agent named by agent:. Pros: every step starts from a clean context. Cons: each step re-reads and re-briefs; the user's conversation sits with the runner, not with the step's agent. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Detached parallel briefs Retro's two mining briefs as two detached steps in one parallel wave. Pros: parallelism declared in the graph. Cons: the lesson set must reach both briefs verbatim from the runner, and the cross-check of their returns is the runner's; detaching would hand the vault lesson read to the agents. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences Retro's prepare is one assisted step issuing two parallel briefs. An external researcher is wired by setting core.research_agent, not by editing a step.","claude-booping:adr/0014":"Context Booping once generated native wrapper agents for external CLI workers through a compile step, beside the path for native agents. The 2026-06-10 retro found the orchestrator confused by that fork — re-invoking a CLI agent it was unsure had run — and drew the takeaway to prefer one homogeneous delegation interface. The same day's Plannotator retro found that a browser review surface attached through existing config extension points with no core change, but that a blanket \"never touch the repo\" invariant forced a contradiction; the narrower intent was \"no Plannotator-specific code in the repo\". When the code-review playbook was authored, an early design gave it a review_surface config key, a named surface agent, its briefing grammar and its fallback exit codes; the user corrected that the playbook knows nothing about Plannotator. Decision An external worker or surface is a self-contained Claude Code agent (global at ~/.claude/agents/ or the user's own), registered under core.{name}_playbook.agents.<id> with good_for/bad_for bullets and steered by a targeted rule on the step that delegates. Core ships no wrapper, compile step or transport for it, and no playbook body names a specific external tool. Code review's present step shows the findings in chat and collects the verdict; a browser round is the configured agent's own business, and with no agent configured the chat presentation is the whole review, not a fallback. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Config-registered agents accepted low high One delegation path for every worker Generated CLI-agent wrappers rejected high medium Retired; a second delegation path A named surface in the playbook rejected medium high Couples a core playbook to one tool Config-registered agents Every worker, internal or external, is an agent invoked the same way. Pros: one mental model for the runner; the user swaps workers in their own tier; core stays free of tool-specific code. Cons: the user writes the agent and its rule; booping never shapes the agent's prompt. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Generated CLI-agent wrappers A compile step renders a wrapper agent per external CLI worker. Pros: an external CLI looks like a native agent. Cons: a non-homogeneous fork in delegation that confused the orchestrator; a build step and its templates to maintain. Complexity: high. Reversibility: medium. A named surface in the playbook The code-review playbook briefs a configured surface agent with a diff ref, a repo path and a findings shape, and routes its unavailable exit codes to a fallback. Pros: not named in the source. Cons: the playbook carries transport and tool details that are userland; a second presentation path to keep. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences core.{name}_playbook.disable_internal_agents: true leaves only the user's agents in a playbook's delegation table. The Plannotator agent and its transport script stay global, outside the repo.","claude-booping:adr/0015":"Context The first groom playbook had four confirm points, each a status: awaiting-framing-confirm, awaiting-design-confirm, awaiting-decomposition-confirm and the approval. The skill-versus-playbook measurements put the playbook's remaining cost in call count — transitions and gate round-trips. The old groom skill had one approval near the end. Decision Each core playbook stops for review once; other user decisions are taken in-step, in prose (booping:rule/0032), without a status of their own. groom — intake posts a framing brief and the user's answers to its scope questions are the confirmation; design is aligned in conversation while drafting; present is the single approval gate, over a human-targeted summary and the full plan. develop — the only stop is confirming the sprint branch name; handing a plan to develop is its approval, and verify's guardrails decide the exit with no user gate. code-review — no gate; the human's verdict on the findings is the approval. retro — the draft's approval at synthesize. migrate — one gate over the whole pending set, not one per migration. setup — no gate; every decision is asked in-step. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict One gate per run accepted low high Fewer round-trips; in-step questions remain A confirm status per phase rejected medium high Groom's first shape; removed One gate per run A run asks in-step and stops once. Pros: fewer transitions and round-trips; the user reviews the finished artifact rather than each intermediate. Cons: a wrong framing is caught by the user's answers to intake, not by a persisted confirmation. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A confirm status per phase Framing, design and decomposition each end in an awaiting-*-confirm status. Pros: each intermediate is confirmed and the confirmation survives a session. Cons: more calls and user waits per run. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences Groom's compact checkpoint is the framing brief posted in chat at intake, in line with rule/0003. Questions a run cannot settle park it in awaiting-clarification rather than adding a gate.","claude-booping:adr/0016":"Superseded by adr/0035 (2026-10-17): groom has no separate review step, and the cross-review step with its config key leaves the graph. Context The groom skill cross-validated plans through an external Gemini call (docs/cross_validation.md, booping-external-llm-call). During skill-era grooms the user twice had to stop repeated re-runs — \"it's not a tool to run after every change\" — and a one-shot rule was written into the cross-validation partial. The groom playbook needed the same review without framework code for one vendor. Decision cross-review is a standalone step between draft-plan and present, run once. It is detached to the agent named by core.groom_playbook.cross_review_agent, which src/config.yaml declares null; the step's summary, detached: and body all gate on that key's truthiness, and null skips the step. Its return is findings-only: - CRITICAL|RISK|NOTE: {finding} — {plan section} lines, or no findings. The rules for disposing of findings sit in the step's summary, because a detached step's body goes to the agent and the runner never reads it. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Config-gated detached step accepted low high One pass, any agent, zero framework code External Gemini call rejected medium high Vendor path in core; replaced for playbook runs A block inside draft-plan rejected low high First playbook shape; became its own step A presence check on a project-only key rejected low high A bare null renders as None Config-gated detached step A step that exists only when an agent is configured. Pros: any agent (for example codex) plugs in through config; one pass by construction; the plan's author and reviewer are different models. Cons: the runner sees only the summary, so the disposal rules must live there. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. External Gemini call The skill's path: a helper calls another vendor's model on the plan. Pros: not named in the source. Cons: vendor-specific code in core; re-runs had to be stopped by a rule. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. A block inside draft-plan A Jinja block at the end of drafting spawns the reviewer when the key is set. Pros: no extra step. Cons: not named in the source beyond its replacement by a standalone step when the graph was reworked. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A presence check on a project-only key config.get('cross_review'), a key with no core default. Pros: nothing in core config. Cons: a key with no default, schema or owner; rendering a bare null into detached: gives None, read as an agent id. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences With no agent configured, the step is skipped entirely and groom runs without a second-model review. 2026-10-17: a benchmark measured the plain double-check prompt on Opus as the best form of the step, and the step is off by default (cross_review_agent: null); groom has no separate review step (adr/0035).","claude-booping:adr/0017":"Context Vault layout changes — flat plan files into plan directories, retro into its own track, renamed metrics keys — had to reach existing vaults. A migration needs an order, a record of what a vault has applied, and a way for booping to notice a vault that is behind. Decision Each migration is a directory migrations/NNN_{slug}/ in the plugin repo whose migration.md carries the prompt and commands handed to the unit applying it, with frontmatter id, title and summary. The frontmatter id is the single source of truth: the NNN_ prefix is a cosmetic sort hint that nothing parses, so directories can be renamed freely. A vault's applied watermark is the .booping marker's latest_migration key (-1 when nothing is applied). The pending set is a config-declared query spec with root: core — so its glob scans the plugin's files, not the vault — filtered by the numeric operator id:gt against booping.latest_migration. Every render surface gates on the watermark: a vault behind the shipped migrations gets a blocking notice to run /playbook migrate. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Frontmatter id as authority accepted low high What the query surface filters and sorts on Directory prefix as authority rejected low high Parsed from a name; frontmatter is queryable One self-contained file per migration rejected low high No room for material beside the prompt Frontmatter id as authority The id lives in frontmatter; the prefix is for humans. Pros: the generic query surface lists, filters and sorts migrations; the approval gate shows each migration's summary without reading bodies; no mismatch detection is needed. Cons: the id appears twice, once only cosmetically; makemigration must write it into frontmatter. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Directory prefix as authority The zero-padded NNN_ prefix is parsed as the id. Pros: the id is visible in a listing. Cons: needs its own parser beside the query surface; frontmatter and prefix could disagree. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. One self-contained file per migration migrations/NNN_{slug}.md with no directory. Pros: fewer paths. Cons: no place for sibling material, the way a playbook step dir carries material next to prompt.md. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences The query surface gained the root: key (core), the numeric id:gt operator and the booping Jinja global. A try-vault-then-core lookup was rejected as ambiguous when both roots match, and a root prefix inside the glob as an invented path microsyntax. id:gt compares numerically, so the -1 sentinel works and id 10 sorts after 9. The makemigration playbook in playbooks/makemigration/ authors a migration at the next id (moved there from a skill, BL-0016).","claude-booping:adr/0018":"Context Each migration assumes the previous one landed. The user wanted each migration run as an isolated, context-fresh unit whose whole instruction is that migration's file, and wanted it revertable on its own. The playbook engine parallelises a repeated subgraph's instances when every inner step is detached:, so the obvious isolated shape would also run migrations concurrently. The user's ruling: \"no parallel migrations. One done - update .booping, commit everything, run next\"; sequence outranks isolation where the two conflict. Decision survey checks for uncommitted work, proposes committing both repo and vault and commits on the user's confirmation — it never refuses to start — then shows the pending set, rendered into the step body by the query, and asks once. The apply subgraph repeats one runner-performed step per pending migration in ascending id order, strictly one at a time. Its body spawns a fresh sub-agent briefed with the migration's location, so no migration body enters the runner's context; the runner then advances latest_migration, commits everything and continues. A migration that cannot complete stops the run with its id, a diagnosis and a proposed remedy, which may be a manual fix; there is no silent rollback and no skipping ahead. The run is ephemeral: the watermark is the only state. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Runner step spawning a fresh agent accepted medium high Sequence and isolation both kept Detached instances rejected low high Instances could run in parallel Runner applies in its own context rejected low high Sequential but not isolated A gate per migration rejected low high One approval over the set instead Runner step spawning a fresh agent The inner step has no detached: key, so the engine runs instances in order; its body delegates the work. Pros: strict order and halt-on-failure from the graph; each migration in a fresh context; the runner owns the watermark and commits. Cons: the step hand-rolls the bootstrap and return contract the framework gives detached steps; its eval suite was to pin that, and evals were skipped. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Detached instances The inner step is detached, so each instance runs in its own agent. Pros: isolation from the framework. Cons: the engine may run instances concurrently, breaking \"N+1 never starts before N landed\" and halt-on-failure. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Runner applies in its own context The runner executes each migration's prompt itself. Pros: sequential with no delegation contract. Cons: every migration body enters the runner's context; no isolation. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A gate per migration The user approves each migration before it runs. Pros: finer control. Cons: explicitly not wanted by the user; the reason is not named in the source. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences A resumed run re-renders its pending set from latest_migration; a break mid-migration is remedied by inspecting the working tree, which survey surfaces. The playbook shipped without eval suites, by the user's decision.","claude-booping:adr/0019":"Context Groom mirrors a plan's status and its clarification questions onto an issue tracker through script hooks on its transitions. playbook-transition writes status: before it runs the hooks, so a hook that fails aborts the transition with the vault already advanced and the tracker behind. Tracker providers need an HTTP client and per-provider code. Decision Every tracker interaction lives in a second CLI, bin/booping-tracker, a separate uv project that keeps tracker concerns and httpx out of booping-python; it reads its config by shelling to booping config-get core.tracker. The hooks tracker-sync and tracker-comment absorb the tracker's exit code and warn on stderr, so an unreachable tracker never fails a transition. The vault is authoritative; booping-tracker sync --artifact … by hand is the reconcile. One driver ships, cli, a receipts-only no-op; an unknown driver name is a hard error. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Hooks absorb tracker failures accepted low high The run never stalls on the tracker A tracker failure fails the transition rejected low high Vault advanced, transition aborted Tracker code inside booping-python rejected low medium Pulls tracker concerns and httpx into the core CLI Hooks absorb tracker failures The hook reports and warns; the transition stands. Pros: the run continues whatever the tracker does; the vault is the single truth. Cons: the tracker can lag until someone re-runs sync. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A tracker failure fails the transition The hook's exit code propagates. Pros: a failure is impossible to miss. Cons: status is written before hooks, so the transition aborts with the vault advanced and the tracker behind. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Tracker code inside booping-python Providers live in the core CLI. Pros: one CLI. Cons: tracker concerns and an HTTP dependency in the framework. Complexity: low. Reversibility: medium. Consequences With no tracker configured, groom behaves as before; the cli driver prints a receipt and nothing more. A real provider is another module behind the same facade, with its token named by api_key_env, never carried in config.","claude-booping:adr/0020":"Context The suite had 744 pytest cases, roughly 400 of them noise-prone: about 270 imported booping.* internals and would die with any rewrite, and 131 cases in one file grepped exact prose sentences out of a render — wording changes broke tests that verified no behaviour. The rendered prose was already covered twice, by committed snapshots and by mdcheck. Two futures were in view, both needing behaviour-level tests: a Rust rewrite of the CLI, for which an end-to-end corpus doubles as a parity harness, and a framework CLI whose product spec is the per-subcommand behaviour contract. Decision Three layers. A contract corpus — one case per language-neutral data file: an inline fixture tree, argv, expected stdout or structural asserts, exit code and file-tree delta — run only through the bin/booping subprocess boundary, living beside the commands it specifies (booping-python/e2e/, booping-tracker/e2e/, runner pytest-txtar). A small unit core for algorithmic hotspots only: wave resolution, --where parsing, config deep-merge, frontmatter edges. Snapshots plus mdcheck own rendered wording and structure. Tests assert structure — exit codes, frontmatter keys, JSON, headings present — never exact prose; internal-import tests are converted when their command is touched and deleted in the same change. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Contract corpus through the CLI accepted medium medium Survives a rewrite; reads as the spec Unit tests over internals rejected low high Coupled to module structure and wording Contract corpus through the CLI Cases drive the real binary and compare its observable effects. Pros: the same corpus can run unchanged under a future Rust runner; cases are readable as the product spec without running them; refactors do not break them. Cons: a subprocess per case; a pilot and a harness to build first. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: medium. Unit tests over internals Tests import modules and assert on functions and rendered strings. Pros: fast and precise for algorithms. Cons: dead weight on any rewrite; prose assertions break on wording and duplicate the snapshot coverage. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences just e2e runs both corpora; --txtar-update rebaselines the selected cases. create is verified in the corpus, not by the unit tests.","claude-booping:adr/0021":"Context Two sprints (35 + 36 SP) built a score-based benchmark harness. The 2026-07-22 retro found it unused: the actual need was regression safety per prompt instruction, for which \"unit test\"-style evals with decomposed assertions (promptfoo/DeepEval pattern) fit better; groom had never challenged the goal. The prompt-hacks audit also wanted a way to prove an instruction can be removed safely. The benchmark harness now lives in a separate repo, driven by a vault playbook. Decision An eval suite sits beside its step (tests.yaml, promptfooconfig.yaml, _fixtures/), with two tiers per fixture: smoke, deterministic script asserts, and regress, llm-rubric judges. A step's test-plan.md covers both tiers; the tier column decides the implementation. Shared asserts, graders and rubric prompts live in playbooks/_lib/. Suites run through just eval|smoke|regress on subscription auth (claude -p), never in CI; each run posts a sticky, advisory PR comment — no commit status, no merge gate. Playbook-authoring's optimizer steps never run evals themselves: a step diagnoses a given report, edits, and returns the next command; the harness runs the tier and owns the loop and its bounds. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Per-step suites, advisory accepted medium high Regression check per instruction Score-based benchmark harness rejected high high Built, then unused for this need Evals as a CI gate rejected medium high Not run in CI Per-step suites, advisory Small suites pinned to one step's contract. Pros: a failing check names the instruction that regressed; the cheap smoke tier runs without judge calls. Cons: suites must be written per step; most authoring runs skipped them, so several core playbooks ship without suites. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Score-based benchmark harness Whole-run scoring across cases and models. Pros: compares models and whole runs. Cons: did not answer whether a given instruction still holds; 71 SP sat idle. Complexity: high. Reversibility: high. Evals as a CI gate Suites run on every push and block the merge on failure. Pros: a regression cannot merge unnoticed. Cons: evals are expensive — too expensive to run on every CI run. A playbook is built first and its prompts optimised against the evals afterwards; the evals are an optimisation tool, not a merge gate (owner, 2026-10-14). Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences Suites exist for learn, playbook-authoring and the shared return contract; code-review, develop, migrate, retro and setup were authored with evals out of scope. The benchmark harness lives in the separate booping-benchmarks repo.","claude-booping:adr/0022":"Context Develop briefs one worker per group of milestones, capped by core.sprint.max_milestones_per_agent: 2. A worker's real limit is its session context; milestone count does not track it, story points roughly do and are the only size signal already in the plan. Every clean comparison in booping-benchmarks (one fixed 3-milestone, 8-SP refactoring plan, local Qwen3.8 27B, 2026-08..09) has one delegation for the whole plan match or beat one delegation per milestone, in less time, with fewer output tokens and a tighter spread: Date Model Whole plan (n) Per milestone (n) Output tokens Report 08-21 27B Q8 95.9 (3), spread 0.4 ≈95.6 (8), spread 6.5 36–47k vs 55–80k reports/20260821-014756-qwen3-8-27b-q8.md 08-23 / 09-03 27B FP8 97.0 (3) 91.9 (3) 54–96k vs 74–76k reports/20260823-002017-…, reports/20260903-044651-… 09-13 27B, new template 96.3 (1) 91.1 (3) 58k vs 75–88k reports/20260913-114521-qwen3-8-27b.md 09-16..17 27B 93.8–97.2 (6) 89.9–92.5 (09-12/13) — reports/20260916-232343-qwen3-8-27b.md The 09-03 against 09-12 pair changed the chat template together with the delegation shape; the 09-13 control isolates the delegation and keeps the 5-point gap. Ten historical plans had a median of about 23 SP over 5–7 milestones; 13 of 59 milestones had sp ≥ 5, and groom ignored the redecompose threshold in 5 of 10 plans. Decision core.sprint.max_sp: 15 replaces max_milestones_per_agent. Milestones pack greedily and contiguously in graph order while the group's sum(sp) <= max_sp, only within one topological wave; a single milestone is always a valid group and is never split. Grouping changes only the briefing; the runner still validates, commits and transitions per milestone. Groom gets a hard gate: a milestone with sp >= redecompose_threshold (5) does not leave grooming. The cap measures review burden, not how hard the work is for the agent: it bounds how much one briefing hands the human to check at once (owner, 2026-10-16), so it is tuned to the reviewer, not to the model. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict SP cap of 15 accepted low high Halves delegations on the median plan Count cap of two rejected low high Cuts well below the measured floor A 10 to 15 band with rounding rejected low high Same group counts as the plain cap SP cap of 15 One knob; greedy packing within a wave. Pros: simulation roughly halves the delegations on the median plan; a warm context avoids re-briefing and repeated repo reads. Cons: quality is measured only at 8 SP per delegation (n=3 against n=3, one plan, one task type); 15 is a ×2 extrapolation; SP measures human effort, not tokens. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high — the knob is cheap to lower. Count cap of two The current rule: up to two milestones per briefing. Pros: not named in the source. Cons: unrelated to context use; more delegations than needed. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A 10 to 15 band with rounding A cap range rather than one number. Pros: not named in the source. Cons: over the historical plans it gave the same group counts as plain max_sp: 15, so the simpler knob won. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences core.sprint.max_sp (default 15) replaced max_milestones_per_agent, and groom keeps a milestone at or over redecompose_threshold from leaving drafting (BL-0015). Planned calibration: re-groom a 31-SP plan with sp-8 milestones and run it as a second benchmark in two groups of about 15. 2026-10-18 (owner): redecompose_threshold (5 SP) is a threshold on one task — a task at it is research and must be split — and never was one on a milestone. A milestone is the sum of its tasks, grouping an isolated large piece of work, capped only by the group budget max_sp. The milestone-level gate this record introduced is a mistake; groom gates tasks at 5 instead, and playbooks/groom/_schemas/milestone.yaml stops holding milestone sp to 1-4. Measured effect of the old gate: 70% of milestones at 3-4 SP and 92% of tasks at 1-2 SP (OI-0027).","claude-booping:adr/0024":"Context Specs need a user surface: set a tier up, settle values, record decisions, check the set for internal conflicts, learn from runs. The owner decided on 2026-10-15 that the user should call one playbook (or just /playbook) and the work should follow from what the user does, and that strong models do better when they understand the system than when they follow a fixed task list. Decision A single specs playbook carries an explanation of every record type and of the format's principles, plus a set of scenarios the user may be in — for example alignment (the user wants to refine the project's values), auto-review (check the specs for internal conflicts), retro (extract lessons so the model does not repeat a mistake), recording a decision or an idea, syncing specs and code. The model reads the dialogue and decides what to do; the scenarios orient it, they do not restrict it. The alignment interview runs only at init, and as a guide for the user to how specs work: values first, then the boundaries of how the model works follow from them. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict A guide with scenarios accepted medium high The model decides from the dialogue A router over fixed actions rejected medium high Binds the model to a menu A guide with scenarios One playbook explains the system and lists scenarios; the model chooses. Pros: follows natural dialogue; uses stronger models better; one entry point. Cons: less predictable than a fixed path; the guide's quality decides the result. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. A router over fixed actions A route step maps the request to one of init, align, record, review, sync, work. Pros: predictable, easy to test per action. Cons: the model is limited to the menu; a request between actions fits badly. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences When planning, groom names the values in play and flags where the plan departs from them, so the user sees what drives a choice and can refine a value (booping:rule/0030). Confirmation: /playbook specs on a repo without specs offers init and the alignment guide; on a repo with specs it acts on the user's request without asking which action to take.","claude-booping:adr/0025":"Context Specs and code drift apart both ways: a record promises behaviour the code lacks, the code does what no record says, a value conflict is open, a [clarify] waits for the owner. The owner wants a log of these that a playbook can take and groom and build on its own (2026-10-15), and preferred storing items as an index over computing them on the fly, since creating an ADR already creates an item to confirm. Decision An open item is a file in the vault, open-items/OI-NNNN.md, with a status (open, planned, done, dropped), the record it concerns (with anchor), its direction (code to spec, spec to code, conflict, clarify) and the plan that works it. Items are created when a record that needs code lands (an ADR with a confirmation line), by a specs sync, by specs check findings and from marks; closed items stay and are filtered. The command is explicit: booping items list lists the open ones (the index), with the booping items subcommands to add and close. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Stored index accepted medium high One list a playbook can take Computed on the fly rejected low high Loses items no record shows Stored index Every item is a file; closed ones are filtered out. Pros: one list across every source; history kept; a playbook works it directly. Cons: an item can go stale against its record. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Computed on the fly Marks, open conflicts and check findings are collected at query time; nothing stored. Pros: never stale. Cons: a gap found by a sync or an ADR's pending confirmation has nowhere to live. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences A specs playbook run can take open items, groom them and build them in the run's autonomy mode (adr/0036). Confirmation: creating an ADR with a confirmation line adds an open item; closing its plan closes the item; booping items list hides it afterwards. 2026-10-15: the store's command is booping items, and items include user requests filed verbatim (source: request/backlog) beside spec gaps (story/0023).","claude-booping:adr/0026":"Context Retro today digs the moments the user corrected the model out of session transcripts. The owner proposed on 2026-10-15 to log them as they happen in groom, develop, code-review and similar steps, so retro and learn get a ready feed. Decision When the user corrects the model — rejects a proposal, rewrites an output, says it is wrong, answers a gate other than the agent suggested — the agent records a short entry, one file in the vault's corrections/ (adr/0037): the step, what the model did, the user's words verbatim, the reason when stated, the artefact and the session id, so retro can pull the surrounding conversation. Retro reads every open entry as its feed and marks what it handled; learn proposes rules from it. A plain confirmation is not a correction. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Log as it happens accepted medium high A ready feed with links Mine the transcripts at retro rejected low high What retro does today Log as it happens A CLI call writes each correction with its session id. Pros: retro starts from a list; the format is held by code; cross-project history. Cons: relies on the agent noticing a correction. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Mine the transcripts at retro Retro reads whole sessions and finds corrections itself. Pros: nothing to do during the run. Cons: expensive in tokens; misses corrections across long sessions. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences Confirmation: a groom run where the user rejects a proposal leaves an open entry in the vault's corrections/ with its session id, and retro lists it.","claude-booping:adr/0029":"Context booping specs site renders specs roots to a static site. The owner wants one index of projects with a site per project (2026-10-15), on the homelab GitLab (Free tier, Pages enabled; its Pages domain resolves to a private LAN address, so access control is not needed). Only projects whose vault lives in the repo have their specs there to build from. Decision Per project. A project whose vault is in its repo adds a pages job that runs booping specs site --out public on every push to its default branch; booping ships the job as a template. Its Pages access is public (LAN only). The site lives at {pages-domain}/<project>. Index. The group's Pages-root project serves the group's Pages root {pages-domain}: one page listing every project's site with its one-line description, generated by code (booping specs site-index). A scheduled pipeline rebuilds it from the group's projects that carry the topic booping-specs, read through the GitLab API with a read-only token kept as a masked CI variable; adding a project to the index is setting the topic. GitLab-specific parts (the CI template, the API discovery) are project and tracker concerns; the core ships the site, the index generator and the template. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Site per project, index by topic accepted medium high Each site builds with its own repo; index needs no edits One aggregated site from the notes repo rejected low high Couples every project to one build Site per project, index by topic Each repo builds its own site; the index only links them. Pros: a project's site updates with its own push; adding a project is a topic. Cons: the index needs an API token; projects with an external vault get no site. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. One aggregated site from the notes repo The notes repo builds one site over every project's specs roots. Pros: one build, covers external vaults too. Cons: one failing project breaks the whole site; rebuilds on every notes change. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences Confirmation: a push to a project with the template publishes {pages-domain}/<project>; setting the topic adds it to {pages-domain} on the next scheduled run.","claude-booping:adr/0030":"Context The current groom playbook (V0) spends 13–23 minutes and $4.25–5.62 per plan across many detached steps. A benchmark in booping-benchmarks/groom (idea/0003) ran variants on six past tasks in isolated environments; round 2 judged 12 plans per task (V2, V3, V3a, V5 × 3) on s1 (small), m2 (medium) and c1 (complex) with codex and a neutral claude -p, no rubric and no final solution, plus a mechanical incidents metric (what a plan would have prevented of what really went wrong). Each variant writes the whole plan as one file. Variant Mean judge score (6 cells) Incidents s1 / m2 Cost / time per plan V3 — template + research → draft → self-review 6.7 73% / 70% $3.09 / 9.1 min V3a — V3 without self-review 6.6 72% / 62% $2.75 / 7.9 min V2 — template only 5.8 70% / 62% $2.92 / 8.9 min V5 — V3 + 5 rules kept from the old groom 5.3 65% / 52% $2.62 / 7.1 min Only the staged variants found m2's hidden defect (the gateway truncates a take at 60 s, confirmed against the code by codex and Gemini); spread inside one variant (V3 scored both 9 and 4 on s1) is larger than between V3 and V3a. A rule ablation on V3 (45 rules in 9 batches, 40 runs, groom/ablation/report.md) found 16 rules the model follows without being told, 16 carried by the template's sections, and 9 milestone-shape rules it misses even when told (36–58%); removing a batch does not change the cost per plan. Criteria Plan quality at least that of V3 on the benchmark tasks. Cost and wall time well under V0's. Decision Groom is one drafting pass on the plan template with two stages, research then draft, and no separate self-review step (V3a). The 16 rules the ablation shows the model follows unprompted and the anti-pattern list leave the prompt; the template's sections stay. The draft is the whole plan in one file, milestones included; how it becomes milestone files is adr/0031. The 9 milestone-shape rules the model misses even when told are not repeated in the prompt either: they are checked after the split (adr/0031). Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict V3a: research then draft accepted medium high Quality of V3 at less cost and time V3 with self-review rejected medium high +0.1 points for +12% cost and +15% time V3 with the old groom's rules rejected low high Last of the four on judges and incidents Keep the current groom rejected low high Twice the time and cost; judged lower on s1 and m2 V3a: research then draft The template plus a research stage over the code and a drafting stage, in one session. Pros: quality on par with V3; about 2× faster and 1.7× cheaper than V0; the shortest prompt. Cons: judged on three tasks with n = 3 per variant; spread inside a variant is large. Complexity: medium — the groom playbook is rewritten around one drafting step. Reversibility: high — the old playbook stays in git. V3 with self-review The same drafting pass with a self-review stage at the end. Pros: highest mean score, best incidents on m2. Cons: the gain over V3a is inside the noise; the step costs money and time on every plan. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. V3 with the old groom's rules V3 plus five rules from the current groom the one-shot variants had missed (scoped Verify, a section shape before improvising, self-contained milestones, assumption lines, a short summary). Pros: names what reviewers asked for before. Cons: last of the four on both judges and incidents; never found m2's defect; the run is the fastest, which suggests the rules pull effort from research into form. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Keep the current groom The multi-step groom playbook as it is (V0). Pros: no work. Cons: 13–23 minutes and $4.25–5.62 per plan; last on s1 and m2 in round-1 judging (codex ranked it first on c1). Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences playbooks/groom is rewritten around one drafting step; its snapshot reports change. The plan template states the one-file milestone format adr/0031 cuts on. A light path for small, already-decided requests is a follow-up (idea/0009, corpus case s3-booping-specs-site-registries).","claude-booping:adr/0031":"Context Groom drafts the whole plan as one file (adr/0030), while develop reads index.md plus one file per milestone (milestones/M{nn}-*/) for per-milestone status, feedback and delegation, and packs milestones into briefings by SP (adr/0022). Something has to turn the draft into that layout without changing the plan's text. The 9 milestone-shape rules the model misses even when told (adr/0030) also need a place other than the prompt. A split benchmark (booping-benchmarks/groom/split/report.md) took 12 single-file plans from the groom benchmark (V3 and V3a on s1, m2, c1) and scored each way of splitting: structure of the files, coverage of the plan's lines, invented lines, SP groups, cost. Way Plans passing every threshold Coverage / invention Cost / time per plan Script on the plan's headings 12 / 12 100% / 0% $0 / under 1 s Sonnet writes the files 12 / 12 99.8% / 0.1% $0.71 / 4.6 min Haiku maps line ranges, a script cuts 8 / 12 99.8% / 0% $0.34 / 3.6 min Haiku writes the files 6 / 12 98.2% / 1.5% $0.25 / 4 min All 12 plans carried milestone headings with Tasks / DoD / References / Verify sub-headings; SP came from frontmatter or the milestone table. Haiku rewriting dropped labels, joined lines and garbled a diagram on long plans; its line maps drifted by 1–2 lines and put DoD under Tasks. Criteria The plan's text reaches the milestone files unchanged. Develop and the worker agents read the same layout as today. Cheap enough to run on every plan. Decision booping plan split cuts the drafted plan on its milestone headings (### M{nn} — <title> with sp:, depends_on: and the sub-headings Tasks, Definition of Done, References, Verify), byte for byte, into index.md and the milestone files; it writes the milestone table and the SP groups (adr/0022) and never splits a code fence. It exits non-zero when the plan has no milestone headings and flags a milestone without sp; only such a plan goes to a model (Opus) for the split. After the split mdcheck checks the milestone contract against its schema (adr/0038) — and a failing milestone goes back to the draft with the finding. The benchmark below measured Sonnet; the fallback runs on Opus, because rewriting a draft is judgement work (rule/0031). Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Script on headings accepted medium high Exact and free on all 12 plans Sonnet writes the files rejected low high Passes, but pays $0.71 a plan for what a script does; a model split, on Opus, is kept as the fallback Haiku maps, a script cuts rejected medium high Line numbers drift; sections land wrong Haiku writes the files rejected low high Edits the plan's text on long plans Script on headings A deterministic cut on the headings the draft prompt fixes. Pros: 100% coverage and 0% invention by construction; free; testable with e2e cases on real plan shapes; failures are explicit exit codes. Cons: the plan format is a hard contract for the drafting model; placement inside a milestone is only as right as the draft's own sub-headings. Complexity: medium — a new command, its tests, the contract check. Reversibility: high. Sonnet writes the files A model reads the plan and writes index.md and the milestone files. Pros: passes every threshold on all 12; needs no fixed format. Cons: $0.71 and 4.6 minutes on every plan; a model copy can still drift. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Haiku maps, a script cuts Haiku returns line ranges per milestone section and the groups; a script slices them. Pros: no rewritten text (0% invention). Cons: no first map met the every-line-once rule; boundaries drift by 1–2 lines, splitting code fences and putting DoD under Tasks; 8/12. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Haiku writes the files The cheapest model rewrites the plan into the files. Pros: cheapest model call. Cons: removes labels, joins lines, rewords assumptions, garbles diagrams on long plans; 6/12. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences New booping plan split, with e2e cases on real plan shapes including the 12 benchmark plans; groom calls it after the draft. _partials/milestone_contract.md turns into the post-split check, an mdcheck schema since adr/0038. Develop, pi-developer, booping-developer and develop-pi keep reading the milestone files as they are. Renaming the milestone file to milestones/M{nn}-*/index.md (owner, 2026-10-16) is a separate change with its own migration.","claude-booping:adr/0032":"Context The owner reads specs on the published site (GitLab Pages, adr/0029), often on a phone, and is the only one who may set reviewed: on a record. The site is static: no server, no login, no write path back to the repo. The first comment feature (a floating Comment button on a selection, § buttons on paragraphs, a header sheet and a \"My comments\" page) was not found by the owner on a real record page (2026-10-16), and it had no way to mark a record reviewed at all. The sidebar's \"Review\" entry already names a different thing: the list of records without a review. Criteria Commenting and marking reviewed are visible on the page without being told how. What reaches the agent is exactly what the owner sees before sending. No server, no credentials in the browser; the repo changes only through an agent. Decision A review panel beside each record (and plan and retrospective pages): always visible on wide screens, a bottom sheet on phones. It keeps a free-form input at the top for a comment on the whole record, and lists the record's comments below it, newest first. Selecting text in the body opens a new comment card with the quote and focuses its input; clicking a comment focuses it, clicking its quote scrolls to the place. A Reviewed checkbox marks the whole record. Outbox — a header button with the count of unsent items opens a page with every comment and reviewed mark on the site, grouped by record (the record with the newest comment first, comments newest first), in the exact text that will be sent, with Copy all and Clear. It replaces \"My comments\". The agent applies the review. The owner pastes the block to an agent; the agent edits the records and sets reviewed: with the date of each <ref> — reviewed <date> line. It checks each item against the record first, so an item sent twice is recognised as done and skipped. Names. The header page is Outbox / «К отправке»; the sidebar list of records without a review becomes Awaiting review / «Ждут ревью». \"Review\" alone names neither. Where the review lives and how it ends. Storage: the browser's localStorage, one key per site (its base URL), so a site rebuild keeps it and two sites never mix. It is per browser and device: nothing syncs, the copied text is the only transfer. In a private window it lasts for that window; clearing site data loses it. Reload: everything is restored — the panel's cards, typed inputs (saved as you type) and the reviewed checkbox. No sent state: Copy all only copies; nothing is marked. The owner clears when done — Clear on the Outbox removes everything, Delete removes one item. Sending an item again is harmless (point 3). Moved text: when a quote no longer appears in the record, the comment stays with its quote and a \"text changed\" note instead of a highlight. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Review panel and Outbox accepted medium high Visible, phone-friendly, the agent stays the only writer Keep the floating affordances rejected low high The owner did not find them; no reviewed mark Comments as tracker issues rejected high medium Needs a token in the browser or a backend; GitHub and GitLab sites differ Edit records in the browser and open a PR rejected high medium Credentials in the browser; skips the agent that keeps records consistent Review panel and Outbox The decision above. Pros: the affordance is on screen, not hidden behind hover; free-form notes need no selection; the Outbox is a preview of exactly what the agent gets; works offline and from file://. Cons: a review lives in one browser until it is copied; a cleared browser loses unsent notes; nothing tracks what was sent, so clearing is the owner's step. Complexity: medium — a panel, a page, the reviewed mark and its badge. Reversibility: high — client-side only; the export format is unchanged. Keep the floating affordances The first comment feature as shipped. Pros: already built. Cons: not discoverable on a real record page; no reviewed mark; two different things called \"Review\". Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Comments as tracker issues Each comment posted to GitHub or GitLab as an issue or a note. Pros: shared between devices; visible to other people. Cons: a token in the browser or a backend for a static site; two trackers with different APIs; the agent still has to apply them to the records. Complexity: high. Reversibility: medium. Edit records in the browser and open a PR An editor on the record page that commits through the forge's API. Pros: the change lands without an agent. Cons: credentials in the browser; the owner edits Markdown by hand, the agent's consistency work (refs, dates, related records) is skipped. Complexity: high. Reversibility: medium. Consequences The site's comment UI is rebuilt around the panel; comments.html becomes the Outbox page. The sidebar entry is renamed; labels in en and ru. The export gains <ref> — reviewed <date> lines; the specs playbook tells the agent how to apply them (set reviewed:, never on its own initiative).","claude-booping:adr/0033":"Superseded by adr/0034 (2026-10-16): the consult mechanism moved there; the list of named points where it runs gave way to the base guidance every playbook carries. Context Specs reach the playbooks one way today: rules with targets: inject their brief into the targeted step (booping:rule/0001, rule/0020). Values, ADRs, stories and references reach no groom or develop step, and no step calls booping specs; only the specs and project-manager playbooks read the registry. So a groom re-decides what an accepted ADR settled, a develop worker picks a default an ADR already answers, and autonomous runs record defaults with no reference to a record. The orchestrator showed the same gap on 2026-10-16: it set out to groom a request whose every decision was already made, wrote adr/0032 after a one-word grep, and its autonomous runs logged defaults without citing records. Reading the registry inline costs context: 104 project records plus core and global ones, and most are irrelevant to any one request (value/0002). Agents already return bounded answers (booping:rule/0009) and the driver owns every vault read and write (rule/0019). booping:rule/0030 makes groom name the values in play and flag departures; idea/0008 (open) proposes ADR drafts for a plan's real forks. Criteria A playbook knows what the specs already decide before it decides. The main context grows by a bounded list, not by records. Departures from a record are visible to the owner, or recorded when no owner is present. Decision Specs consult. A read-only subagent (booping-researcher) gets the question and the registry's index (booping specs ls, one summary line per record across every tier), opens only the records whose summary bears on it, and returns at most 15 lines in four groups: decided (records that already settle part of it, ref and how), contradicted (records the request or plan goes against, ref and why), values in play (refs), silent (what no record covers). Where it runs. groom: once at intake, on the request; again in the draft for each fork the draft meets. develop: only when a worker hits a decision the plan does not make, before any default. code-review: once per review, on the diff's intent, to check it against accepted ADRs and rules. orchestrator and project-manager: before routing a request (groom, direct brief or a record first) and before self-accepting a decision in an autonomous run. What happens with the answer. Decided is cited in the plan or the defaults file instead of being re-decided. Contradicted is shown to the owner at the next review gate, or in an autonomous run recorded with the ref and a [clarify] marker and never silently overridden. Values in play feed booping:rule/0030, whose targets widen from groom to develop and code-review. Silent is where a new record may be due: a real fork goes to idea/0008 (still open; this ADR gives it a trigger, not its design). After develop, the driver updates the records the sprint touched: an ADR's status, a story it delivered, a spec-gap issue where the code now departs from a record. The behaviour reaches each playbook as targeted rules (rule/0020), not as edits to every prompt. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Subagent consult accepted medium high Bounded context, reads only what bears on the question Index inline in the prompt rejected low high Every step pays for the whole index and judges relevance itself Deterministic retrieval rejected medium high Tags and paths miss records about the same decision in other words Rule briefs only rejected low high Today's state: ADRs and values never reach the steps Subagent consult The decision above. Pros: the main context gets 15 lines; the subagent reads bodies only when summaries say so; one return contract for every caller. Cons: a subagent call per consult (cost and a little latency); its judgement of relevance can miss a record. Complexity: medium — the consult brief, targeted rules per playbook, the after-develop update. Reversibility: high — rules can be retargeted or retired. Index inline in the prompt Every step receives booping specs ls and reads what it wants. Pros: no extra call. Cons: a hundred-plus lines in every step's context, growing with the registry; the step itself must judge relevance mid-task. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Deterministic retrieval booping specs relevant <paths|tags> picks records by tags, sources and touched paths. Pros: free and repeatable (value/0015). Cons: records are prose about decisions; a request in other words or a new path matches nothing. Useful later as the subagent's pre-filter, not as the consult itself. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Rule briefs only Keep injection of rule briefs as the only path. Pros: no work. Cons: values, ADRs and stories stay invisible to groom and develop; decisions repeat. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences A consult brief in src/ (the return contract above) and targeted rules for groom, develop, code-review, project-manager; booping:rule/0030's targets widen. The orchestrator's own process follows the same rule (consult before routing and before self-accepting); another project's CLAUDE.md line on updating specs/ moves into the core as point 4. A measurable check: re-groom corpus case s3-booping-specs-site-registries and see whether the consult reports its decisions as already made.","claude-booping:adr/0034":"Context Specs stay current only if they are maintained during the work and used where they help (value/0016). Today a playbook knows about them only through rule briefs injected into its steps (rule/0020, booping:rule/0001); only the specs playbook reads the format guide (adr/0024). So spec material the owner states in passing — a value before a groom, a usage scenario, a decision — stays in the transcript, and a run that decides does not know what is already settled. adr/0033 proposed a consult at named decision points; it still depended on a playbook knowing that specs exist and what they are for, and it named its points one by one. Writing a scenario per playbook (\"groom does X with stories, develop does Y with ADRs\") would not cover the next playbook and would not cover what the owner says outside those scenarios. Criteria Any playbook, including one written later, works with the specs without a scenario written for it. The main context grows by a small fixed block. What a playbook writes into the specs is a draft the owner confirms (value/0001). Decision A principle, not a set of scenarios. Every playbook — core, global or project — runs with a base guidance on the specs: What it knows. What the specs are for and what each record type holds (values, rules, conflicts, ADRs, stories, ideas, references, runbooks), well enough to recognise spec material in its own work and in what the owner says; and the values of the project and global tiers, one line each, to orient on without a lookup. It does not hold rules, ADRs or other records. How it gets it. Rendered with the project context the playbook skill already renders for every run, from the specs format guide and the values' summaries; the size is fixed and checked. How it works with them. It applies the specs where they add quality to its result or knowledge of decisions already made, and skips them where they add nothing. It updates them as the work happens: when its work or the owner's words produce spec material — a decision, a scenario, a departure from a record, a record the work made untrue — a draft or a candidate lands in the right place. Values and anything that drops or weakens a rule stay owner drafts; every other draft follows the run's autonomy mode (adr/0036). Through a subagent. Every read or write of the specs goes through a subagent, and the playbook keeps only its short answer. A departure from a record is shown to the owner, or recorded with the ref and a [clarify] marker in an autonomous run, never silently overridden. Where and when a playbook does this is its own judgement under this guidance. A targeted rule for a playbook is added only after a measured miss (rule/0030) — first check: re-groom corpus case s3-booping-specs-site-registries with the guidance alone. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Base guidance for every playbook accepted low high Covers every playbook and every unscripted moment at a fixed small cost Scenarios per playbook rejected medium high Covers only what was foreseen, rots as playbooks change Mine transcripts after the fact rejected medium high Late and lossy; at most a second net in retro Full registry in every session rejected low high Context cost rejected in adr/0033 Base guidance for every playbook The decision above. Pros: one mechanism for core, global and project playbooks; the model applies a principle instead of matching a scenario; the values steer every run. Cons: judgement can over-fire (drafts for chatter) or miss; the subagent's draft and the owner's confirmation are the filter. Complexity: low — a rendered block, the consult and capture briefs, a size check. Reversibility: high. Scenarios per playbook Each playbook gets rules telling it which records to read and write at which step. Pros: precise where foreseen. Cons: every new playbook needs its own; what the owner says outside a scenario is lost; rules multiply. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Mine transcripts after the fact A retro or nightly step reads session logs and proposes records. Pros: catches what a run missed. Cons: runs after the decisions were made; the context of a sentence is gone. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Full registry in every session Every run loads the index or the records. Pros: no subagent call. Cons: hundreds of lines per run; rejected in adr/0033. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences The playbook skill's project-context render gains the guidance block (format summary, the values, the read-and-write principle); a snapshot covers its size. The specs playbook's guide step accepts a handed-over piece of spec material and returns a short result, so any playbook can use it as the capture subagent. A check: run the capture subagent over this conversation (2026-10-16) and compare what it records with what the owner said.","claude-booping:adr/0035":"Context The groom refactor set out to make grooming faster and better at once (idea/0003). The one-pass draft (adr/0030) already did it: about 8 minutes a plan against 13–23 for the old groom, at equal or better judged quality. Groom keeps a config-gated cross-review step (adr/0016); on the owner's machine it ran codex. A benchmark then measured review as a separate step (booping-benchmarks groom/xreview, 2026-10-17): 36 plans (V2, V3, V3a, V5 on s1, m2, c1), each reviewed, folded in by Opus and judged against a V3a reference by codex and a neutral claude -p, on readiness and on how well the plan challenges the request; the codex judge scored 22 of 36 plans before its usage limit. Review Readiness effect (codex / claude-p) Challenge effect (codex / claude-p) Verdict Fresh Opus, plain \"double-check\" prompt +1.77 / +1.22 +2.27 / +1.42 helps on challenge Fresh Opus, cross-review prompt +1.82 / +0.89 +1.77 / +0.92 no measurable effect codex, cross-review prompt +0.91 / +0.44 +1.17 / +0.03 no measurable effect Gemini, cross-review prompt (8 plans) +0.50 / +0.38 +0.62 / −0.38 no measurable effect Head to head on nine pairs, V3a plus a fresh Opus double-check beat V3 (self-review in the drafting session) by +1.00 / +0.56 on readiness and +1.78 / +1.67 on challenge, and brought incident coverage from V3a's 71% to V3's 79%. No reviewer found the hidden defect of task m2 (a 60 s gateway cap) on any plan whose draft missed it. The review and its fold-in added 10.4 minutes a plan on average (review 4.6, fold-in 4.6 in a separate session); folding in inside the drafting session and reviewing without reading the code could bring it to about 4–5 minutes, unmeasured. Criteria Groom stays fast: the point of the refactor was less time and better quality together. A step earns its time by what it changes in the plan the owner then executes. Decision Groom runs no separate review step, and the cross-review step leaves the graph together with its cross-reviewing status and the core.groom_playbook.cross_review_agent key. The one-pass draft is the plan; its only check after drafting is the deterministic mdcheck milestone check (adr/0030, adr/0038). The measured gain of a fresh review is real but not worth 10 minutes a plan, and not worth 4–5 either: the refactor already reached its goal of shorter time at equal or better quality, and adding the review back would trade most of the time saved for a gain inside the plans' own spread (owner, 2026-10-17). The owner asked for no separate step; keeping the step behind its config key was a drafting addition, dropped on 2026-10-17 (owner). Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict No review step accepted low high Keeps the time the refactor won Fresh Opus double-check rejected low high Better plans, about 10 minutes more each Optimised fresh review rejected medium high 4–5 minutes, still too much for the gain Codex cross-review rejected low high No measurable effect No review step The decision above. Pros: about 8 minutes a plan; nothing to configure. Cons: forgoes about +1 point of readiness and +1.5 of challenge measured on 36 plans. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high — one config key turns a review back on. Fresh Opus double-check A separate Opus session gets the request, the plan and the code, and lists what is wrong, missing or will break; the drafting session folds the findings in. Pros: the only arm with a measured gain; challenges the request better than any other. Cons: 10.4 minutes a plan as measured; misses what the draft's research missed (m2's cap). Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Optimised fresh review The same review without reading the code, folded in by edits inside the drafting session. Pros: an estimated 4–5 minutes. Cons: unmeasured; part of the findings may come from the code; still half the draft's time. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Codex cross-review The step as it ran before the benchmark. Pros: fast (about 4 minutes with fold-in). Cons: no measurable effect on either metric; many CRITICAL findings to dispose of. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences The cross-review step, the cross-reviewing status, its edges and cross_review_agent are removed from groom; the owner's machine config drops the key. adr/0016 is superseded by this record. adr/0030 stays: the one-pass draft without self-review is the groom.","claude-booping:adr/0036":"Context The owner works two ways: supervised in work projects, where every gate waits for review, and autonomous in pet projects and experiments, where the manager grooms and builds on its own. Whether a run waits for the user is a property of the session that runs it. A session told \"work autonomously\" already works that way, and booping run … --autonomous already hands every child run that instruction through core.run.preamble, including the escalation path: a question only the owner can answer parks the run, and the manager reports it as waiting on the user. Decision Autonomy is settled in prose. A run is autonomous when the user says so to the session, or when it was launched with booping run … --autonomous; otherwise it is supervised. The project manager reads the mode from the user's words and passes it on through --autonomous, the only switch. The driving protocol and the step bodies carry both branches as prose (\"in an autonomous run … , otherwise …\"). No config key chooses the mode (owner, 2026-10-18). Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Prose accepted low high One switch, already proven by the launch preamble A config key rejected medium high A second switch that did not hold anyway Prose The user's words or the launch preamble decide; the bodies describe both behaviours. Pros: one switch; the mode follows the conversation, not a file; nothing to validate or render per mode. Cons: both branches sit in every driving context; the model, not code, reads which applies (a departure from value/0015). Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A config key core.autonomy: supervised | autonomous per project, picking the protocol's branch at render and stopping on any other value. Pros: the mode is chosen by code; a project keeps its default without restating it. Cons: a second switch beside --autonomous; a supervised project's child runs already got the supervised text plus the autonomous preamble, and the preamble won, so the key bought nothing the prose did not. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences core.autonomy, its invalid-value STOP, the autonomy: project-context line and their e2e cases are removed. _playbook_driving.j2, the project-manager and specs playbooks render no mode branch; they say what an autonomous run does in prose. migrate's question about uncommitted work is asked in every run, autonomous or not. Every gate and question an autonomous run settles is still logged as an agent decision (adr/0037).","claude-booping:adr/0037":"Context Three append-only journals had their own commands: booping correction add|list (a transcript lookup proving the quote, a dialogue link), booping defaults add and booping items ask|answer (an open/answered marker and a two-part answered test) — about 550 lines and 35 e2e cases. Code reads two of them: retro's prompt is filled with the open corrections at render time, and the manager reviews the open agent decisions. Each entry also needs the time and the session id, facts outside the model's view (rule/0025). Item questions are read by booping status only to say an item waits on the user; plan questions are read by the groom driver when it resumes and posted verbatim to the tracker. Building nothing — retro mining transcripts — was rejected by adr/0026 for its token cost and loses the review of agent decisions. Criteria What code reads comes from code; what only a model reads stays prose (rule/0025). No new command where an existing primitive does the job. Decision A journal entry is one vault file with YAML frontmatter, built from primitives booping already has. booping create core.<journal>.template <dir> --set name=… --set … writes it from a config template, whose macros stamp the time and the session id; booping frontmatter-update moves its state; booping query slices entries by frontmatter, at render time inside a template or on the command line. corrections/ — step, did, said, why, state: open | handled; retro takes every open entry and marks what it handled. agent-decisions/ (was defaults) — step, asked, decision, why, state: open | reviewed. An open item's question is its status: the item machine gains clarify (open → clarify when it asks, clarify → open when answered), moved by booping playbook-transition --state open_item; the question and the answer are prose in the item's body. booping status lists clarify items as waiting on the user. A plan's questions stay prose in its clarifications.md by the format doc; the plan's awaiting-clarification status is what waits on the user. There is no journal command, no field DSL and no entry parser. Obsidian reads the entries as notes and can table them with a Base, as it does plans. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Entries as files over existing primitives accepted low high No new code; every reader is an existing command One journal command rejected medium high A second way to store records and a fragile parser Entries as open items rejected low high Corrections would enter the work queue One prose file per journal rejected low high Code reads the slices; the model cannot stamp time and session Entries as files over existing primitives The decision above. Pros: no new code; create, frontmatter-update and query are already tested; entries are native vault notes. Cons: many small files; an entry's id is its file name, given by the caller; fields are not validated. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. One journal command booping journal add|set|list over core.journals.<name> with typed fields, entries as H2 sections of one file per journal; built and then discarded on 2026-10-18. Pros: one readable file per journal; short sequential ids; enum values checked. Cons: about 200 lines and a field DSL; an entry parser that breaks on **Label:** or ## inside a text field; records stored differently from every other vault record. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Entries as open items A correction or a decision is an open item with a kind. Pros: id allocation, states and listing already exist. Cons: open items are the manager's work queue; excluding corrections and decisions from it is new code, and an item's lifecycle does not fit a log entry. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. One prose file per journal The driver appends entries by a format doc, with no command at all. Pros: nothing to build or keep. Cons: retro's render-time slice and the manager's review are read by code; the model does not know the time or its session id. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences booping journal, core.journals and docs/journals.md are removed with the commands they replaced; the vault-root corrections.md and agent-decisions.md give way to the two directories. The open item machine gains clarify; open-items/questions/ goes. clarifications.md keeps its prose format doc; groom writes it by hand. The repo guide lists what each booping subcommand can do, not only its name: given names only, a fresh model generalised the old journal commands into a new one; told what scaffold, query and frontmatter-update do, it chose this decision every time (reference/0009).","claude-booping:adr/0038":"Context The model misses milestone-shape rules even when told (adr/0030), so groom's gate needs a check that code runs. booping plan check did it in 143 lines of its own: one Verify command, a path in Tasks, at least as many DoD checkboxes as tasks, sp below core.sprint.redecompose_threshold. mdcheck already checks every specs record against a schema. A schema with the same four rules, run over the 218 milestones in this vault, reported every finding plan check reported except the checkbox-to-task comparison, which mdcheck cannot express; it also flagged 59 fenced Verify blocks, which plan check accepted alongside a single inline code span. Building nothing means prose rules, which adr/0030 measured missing. Decision The milestone contract is playbooks/groom/_schemas/milestone.yaml, and groom runs mdcheck <schema> {plan-dir}/milestones/*/M*.md directly. The contract narrows to what the schema can state: Verify is one inline code span as its first paragraph, DoD holds at least one checkbox, each task's SP is 1–4 and the milestone sp, their sum, is at most 15 (corrected 2026-10-18: the 5 SP threshold is a task's, never a milestone's — adr/0022). booping plan check and its code are removed. mdcheck becomes a required dependency of every install, not only of projects with specs. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict mdcheck schema run directly accepted low high Reuses the checker specs already depend on A booping check wrapper rejected medium high A command with no reader beyond what mdcheck prints Keep plan check rejected low high 143 lines for what a schema states mdcheck schema run directly The decision above. Pros: no booping code; one checker and one rule language for records and milestones; findings carry line numbers. Cons: a required install step for every user; the DoD-to-task comparison and the config-driven sp threshold are lost; fenced Verify blocks become findings. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A booping check wrapper booping check <path> picks the schema by the file's type and runs mdcheck, folding specs check in. Pros: one command for every structural check; could read the threshold from config. Cons: new code whose only reader is the model reading mdcheck's own findings. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Keep plan check The command as it was. Pros: the exact checks, no new dependency. Cons: its own parser for sections, fences and lists, beside a checker that already does it. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences Groom's draft-plan, present and gate text name the mdcheck call; the install guide lists mdcheck as required. Plans written before 2026-10-18 with fenced Verify blocks fail the schema; they are history and are not re-checked.","claude-booping:adr/0039":"Context Core rules booping:rule/0032 and booping:rule/0033 and the specs guidance reach an agent only through a rendered playbook, agent or skill body. A session that works on a project outside any playbook never sees them. On 2026-10-18 a session in another booping project wrote two ADRs as accepted and dispatched developers without showing the owner the decision, its alternatives or the ADR it partly reversed; it saw the /playbook skill in its list and did not load it. The same day a session in this repo proposed a new record status without checking the existing reviewed field. Building nothing lets both errors repeat. The plugin ships no hook today. Criteria A session doing project work a playbook fits learns that it should go through it, before it takes decisions. Nothing changes outside booping projects. The playbook list is rendered from the source the /playbook skill already uses, never restated (value/0003). Decision The plugin ships a SessionStart hook. When no .booping marker is found it injects nothing. When one is found it injects: a short prose block on when to call a playbook — project work a playbook fits goes through it; the rendered playbook table, from the same source as the /playbook skill. The agent offers the fitting playbook in the dialogue; when nothing narrower fits, the project-manager is the default offer (owner, 2026-10-19). Loading a playbook brings its rules with it, so the core rules reach the session the way they reach a run. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict SessionStart hook in booping projects accepted low high Tells every session in a booping project where its work belongs, before any decision Triggers in the skill description only rejected low high Already failed: the session saw the skill and did not load it Load the project-manager on every session start rejected low high Every message would go through intake into items and gates, debugging and questions included A narrower hook: prompt topic or spec writes rejected medium high Keyword matching is fragile; a write hook fires after the decision is taken SessionStart hook in booping projects The decision above. Pros: reaches every session in a booping project, including one that never thinks of /playbook; silent everywhere else; the table is rendered from the existing source; the agent still chooses in dialogue, so a debugging question is not forced into a playbook. Cons: about 2–3 KB of context in every session of a booping project, used or not — a departure from value/0002; the first hook the plugin ships, a new surface to maintain and test; an offer can still be ignored. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Triggers in the skill description only Sharpen the /playbook skill description so the model loads it when project work starts. Pros: nothing to build; no context cost beyond the skill list. Cons: prose in the description already failed on 2026-10-18 — the session saw the skill and did not load it. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Load the project-manager on every session start The hook loads the manager playbook itself, so every session is the manager. Pros: the strongest guarantee that project work goes through the playbooks. Cons: every message — a debugging session, a question — goes through intake into items and gates; a project that wants it can say so in its own CLAUDE.md. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Rejected by the owner on 2026-10-19 with the plan: a project that wants the manager at start says so in its CLAUDE.md. A narrower hook: prompt topic or spec writes A UserPromptSubmit hook matching the prompt's topic to a playbook, or a PreToolUse hook on writes into a specs tier. Pros: context only when it seems relevant. Cons: keyword matching is fragile; a write hook fires after the decision is already taken, too late to show the owner anything. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences The plugin gains its first hook; its output must be reproducible and covered like any render (rule/0027). Every session in a booping project carries the injected block — a recorded departure from value/0002, paid for value/0016 and value/0013. The injected block is the two-column table, Playbook | Trigger — about 3 KB on this repo (plan 202610190033, decision 16).","claude-booping:adr/0040":"Context A record's reviewed: is a date the owner sets, by hand or through the specs site's Outbox (adr/0032). It says the owner read the record, nothing more: it cannot tell a brief the owner was shown from a record discussed at length or one explicitly accepted, and it carries no evidence anyone can check. ADR acceptance does not wait for an explicit yes — the agent shows a brief and asks for objections (owner, 2026-10-19) — so how deeply a record was reviewed has to be visible on the record itself. Today no record in this repo carries reviewed:. Decision Review is an axis separate from status, with four levels (owner, 2026-10-19): none — the owner has not seen it; shown — the agent showed the brief and the owner raised no objection; discussed — there was a discussion, without an explicit yes; accepted — the owner explicitly accepted it. The level replaces reviewed: {date}. accepted carries evidence: the owner's words and where they were said (session, date), for example «А тут вроде ок. газ», so the acceptance can be checked (owner, 2026-10-19). The name shown is the agent's proposal: it names what the agent can prove — it showed the brief — not what the owner did (\"witnessed\", \"seen\"), which the agent cannot prove. Frontmatter: two flat keys, review: {level} and review_evidence: \"{quote} — {date}, {where}\" — flat so Obsidian shows them as columns. An agent may set a level, only with its evidence; never accepted without a quote of the owner. reviewed: becomes a booping specs check error naming the replacement, with no migration (no known record carries it); the site Outbox line becomes {ref} — accepted {date}. shown is named by what the agent can prove: it showed the brief. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Review levels with evidence accepted medium high Shows how deep the owner's review went and lets anyone check an acceptance A new status between proposed and accepted rejected low medium Mixes the record's lifecycle with the owner's engagement Keep reviewed as a date rejected low high Cannot tell shown from discussed from accepted; no evidence The agent-decisions journal rejected low high Autonomous runs only, and it lives in the vault, not on the record Review levels with evidence The decision above. Pros: the depth of review is visible on the record; an acceptance can be checked against the owner's words; it fits acceptance by brief and no objection. Cons: a second axis beside status to keep consistent; the guide, the schemas, the checker, the site's review panel and Outbox all change; the evidence quote may carry another project's words into a public record (rule/0033). Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. A new status between proposed and accepted An assumed status for a record the agent acts on before the owner accepts it. Pros: one field; the status already shows on every listing. Cons: mixes the lifecycle of the decision with how far the owner engaged; every type's status set changes; the existing reviewed field is already the axis for the owner's engagement. Complexity: low. Reversibility: medium. Keep reviewed as a date Leave reviewed: {date} as it is. Pros: nothing to change. Cons: cannot tell a shown brief from a discussion from an explicit acceptance; carries no evidence. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. The agent-decisions journal Record each acceptance as an entry under agent-decisions/ (adr/0037). Pros: the mechanism exists. Cons: it is written in autonomous runs only, and the entry lives in the vault, not on the record it is about. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences The specs guide's reviewed entry, the list of keys a tier schema may not rename, and the site's reviewed mark from adr/0032 change with the level; adr/0032 is partly reversed in its reviewed date. The specs playbook's \"never set reviewed\" line becomes the level rule above.","claude-booping:adr/0041":"Context The specs playbook scores every drafted ADR with the booping:adr-scorer agent before its brief (booping:rule/0036). Today the agent gets the ADR text and the rubric only, and scores the alternatives 0–3. On 2026-10-18 the owner deleted six ADRs as not-ADRs — records that were a rule, a value or nothing — which the scorer had not flagged: judging the text alone, it cannot tell that a record belongs to another type or restates a record that exists, and it cannot name a cheap option that is an existing primitive it has never seen. The options were measured on 12 labelled ADRs, the 6 the owner deleted and 6 kept (private note, not published). Routing counts are the deleted ADRs sent to the type they fit, out of 6. Cheap options: building nothing is option A; prose is option B, the guide's own tests pasted into the prompt; extending an existing primitive is the inventory, the ref — title: summary lines booping specs ls already prints. Criteria Routes a not-ADR to the type it fits, or to none. Names the cheap option an ADR left out. Cost per ADR, in tokens and in time. Decision Option C (owner, 2026-10-19: \"С, очевидно, надо брать\"). The scorer's prompt carries, beside the ADR text and the rubric: the specs guide excerpt — Types and When a record is worth writing; the tier's record inventory — one ref — title: summary line per value, rule and ADR, holding only the records that exist when the ADR is written; the project's primitives. It runs on Opus and keeps tools: ListAgents — it reads nothing beyond its prompt; its body stops saying it has no tools. It replies with one JSON line: route, route_why, overlaps (at most 3 bare refs), alt_score (0–3), missing (at most 3, each naming the existing primitive or record). The gate: route ≠ adr or alt_score ≤ 1 → block; otherwise pass, with missing in the brief as \"Не взвешено:\" and overlaps in \"Задевает:\". The list, not the score, is the signal: with one cheap option cut from a good ADR, the cut option was named in missing 4 times of 4 and the score dropped once of 4. Cost per ADR is about 7K tokens in and 650 out on Opus. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Guide and record inventory on Opus accepted medium high Routes 4.5/6 and catches 6/6 at about 7K tokens an ADR The rubric, with or without the guide excerpt rejected low high Text alone cannot route; the guide alone routes 2.5/6 Guide and inventory on Sonnet rejected medium high Cheaper, but misses 3/6 Opus with read access to the repo rejected low high Costs about ×6 and routes 1/6 Guide and record inventory on Opus Option C: the decision above. Pros: routes 4.5/6 and catches all 6 deleted ADRs; names a cut cheap option 4/4; the inventory comes from a command that exists; the agent still reads nothing beyond its prompt. Cons: the prompt grows to about 7K tokens; the inventory must be rendered at the ADR's creation time, filtered by created_at, or later records leak in; the routing calls on the deleted set are still 4.5 of 6. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. The rubric, with or without the guide excerpt Option A is today's scorer, the ADR text and the rubric: building nothing. Option B adds the guide's Types and When a record is worth writing, prose only. Pros: A costs nothing more; B is a pasted excerpt, no new input to render. Cons: A cannot route at all; B routes 2.5/6; neither knows which records and primitives exist, so neither can name an overlap or a missing primitive. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Guide and inventory on Sonnet Option D: option C's input on Sonnet. Pros: cheaper and faster per ADR than Opus. Cons: misses 3 of the 6 deleted ADRs. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Opus with read access to the repo Option E: Opus finds the records and primitives itself with Read, Glob and Grep. Pros: no input to render; it sees whatever it reads. Cons: costs about ×6 of option C and routes 1/6; opens the agent's tool list, which today reads nothing beyond its prompt. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. Consequences The specs playbook's Record scenario renders the inventory and the primitives into the scorer prompt, gates on route and alt_score, and adds \"Не взвешено:\" to the brief. The adr-scorer agent body and the rubric's output contract change to the JSON line; its eval fixtures stay booping-only, since the repo is public (rule/0033). [clarify] The record is accepted on the owner's choice of option C; the plan that builds it, adr-scorer v3, is not yet developed.","claude-booping:adr/0042":"Context ADR 0039 made the session-start hook inject the playbook table and tell the session to offer the fitting playbook, the project-manager when nothing narrower fits. In a live session on 2026-10-19 the agent picked the right playbook but neither ran it nor offered it properly, and gathered the project status by hand instead: the routing was right, the hand-off never happened. Someone has to route an opening request to a playbook and then carry the work through. The candidates are the session itself (the hook's prose), the /playbook skill, a dedicated entry playbook, a config key, or the project-manager. A screen on 2026-10-19 measured routing alone: one Opus call per request, 18 opening requests, isolated claude -p with no tools, JSON output (private note, not published). The bare /playbook router chose the expected playbook for 16 of 18, the other 2 acceptable alternatives (a feature routed to groom, a typo fix to no playbook); with a targeted rule added, 18 of 18. Neither asked which playbook to run. Not measured: whether the session actually invokes the playbook rather than doing the work by hand, repeats, and hard or ambiguous requests. Cheap options: prose in a prompt is option A (today's hook) and option B (a targeted rule on the skill); extending an existing primitive is option E (the project-manager playbook gains a route) and option D (the config tiers); building nothing is option A. Criteria A project-work request reaches a playbook that runs, not just an offer. The work is managed past the first playbook — groom → develop → review → merge — not routed once and dropped. No new code for the entry now (rule/0025: prose first, a CLI over it is a later optimisation). Context cost per session entry. Decision Option E (owner, 2026-10-19: «мы всегда запускаем PM»). Every request in a booping project session enters through the project-manager playbook — questions, explanations and debugging included; there is no no-playbook exception (owner: «иначе это все будет работать мимо спека, мимо известной информации»). The project-manager is the smarter router: it routes, manages the work, and launches the specific playbooks itself — groom, develop, code-review, specs, retro, docs, pr-description, backlog-manage, model-benchmark, playbook-authoring, migrate and the rest — and orchestrates them (owner: «это его задача»). The change to the manager is OI-0036. For now the wiring is prose: the session-start hook tells the session to run /booping:playbook project-manager on every request; no new code for a direct entry. Rendering the manager and the playbook table straight into session start is a later optimisation, OI-0037, done only once the manager entry proves itself. Why (owner, 2026-10-19): Many projects run as a series of sprints, plus other work such as updating the specs. Narrow entries make every session a separate session for one narrow task. Working with a project manager is far more convenient: one contact the owner talks everything through with, who organises the work — \"groom this task\", or \"groom a scope bigger than one sprint and start it right away\" — grooms, and escalates to the owner when needed. The owner no longer drives tasks that are already being handled. Stricter work goes through the manager too — \"research this carefully, groom, I review, first iteration, ping me, second iteration…\": the meta-level of the work moves to the manager. The manager knows the specs and how to work with them out of the box: it escalates a conflict it sees in a decision, files open items for the tails left behind, and given \"I have an hour\" walks the open items and the specs, collects the conflicts and surfaces the critical ones for the owner to answer. Observed on 2026-10-19: a session that chatted without running a playbook had not read the specs or the details and its suggestions were poor; once the playbook ran, quality rose sharply. So the manager starts right away; about 5K tokens per entry is not a cost worth weighing against that. The manager's handling of edge cases is tuned later, instead of tuning plain routing. This record supersedes ADR 0039 (owner: «мы переосмыслили это»): its hook and its table stay; its \"offer the fitting playbook\", its \"questions and debugging need no playbook\" and its rejection of \"load the project-manager on every session start\" are reversed. Alternatives Option Status Complexity Reversibility Verdict Hook routes straight to the narrow playbooks rejected low high Did not work in practice; routing without orchestration The /playbook skill routes, with a targeted rule rejected low high Routing only, no orchestration A dedicated entry: an intro playbook or a config key rejected medium high Routing only, no orchestration Always the project-manager accepted medium high One contact that routes, orchestrates and escalates; meaningful work starts at once Hook routes straight to the narrow playbooks Option A, the state before this record (ADR 0039): the hook tells the session to offer the fitting playbook and run it once the user agrees, else offer the project-manager. Building nothing. Its variants fall under the same verdict: hook prose that runs the fitting narrow playbook straight away instead of offering it, and a split entry where single-shot requests (a retro, docs, a PR description) go straight to their playbook and only plan-track or unclear work goes to the manager. Pros: nothing to build; the cheapest per entry — no manager render, no status call; a narrow request goes straight to its playbook. Cons: did not work in practice — in a live session on 2026-10-19 the agent named the right playbook but neither ran nor offered it and gathered status by hand; the variants route without orchestrating, so every session narrows to one task (owner). Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. The /playbook skill routes, with a targeted rule Option B: the /playbook skill is the router, sharpened by a skill:playbook rule — no narrow match or several playbooks → project-manager; questions, explanations, debugging → no playbook. Pros: prose only, on an existing primitive; the screen routed 16/18 bare and 18/18 with the rule, never asking which playbook. Cons: routing only, no orchestration (owner): adding orchestration is what improves the experience, because meaningful work starts at once; the screen also measured the routing answer only, not whether the session invokes the playbook. Complexity: low. Reversibility: high. A dedicated entry: an intro playbook or a config key Option C: a new intro playbook as the session's entry, routing to the others. Option D: a key core.entry_playbook naming the playbook the hook sends project work to. Pros: C is an entry shaped only for routing, light to render; D is overridable per tier today, unlike rules, which have no cross-tier override, so a project or user could pick its own entry. Cons: routing only, no orchestration (owner); C is also a new playbook to author and test, D a new config key. Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Always the project-manager Option E: the decision above. Pros: one contact that routes, organises and carries the work through groom, develop, review and merge, escalating to the owner when needed (value/0019); knows the specs out of the box; meaningful work starts at once; reuses an existing playbook; the hook change is prose only. Cons: the manager today classifies only tiny fix, plan, question and decision, so until OI-0036 narrow requests — a retro, docs, a PR description, a benchmark — are squeezed into those classes; about 5K tokens per entry, the manager render plus a booping status call (value/0002) — not worth weighing against the quality gain (owner); two routing tables, the skill's and the manager's, until OI-0037 merges them (value/0003). Complexity: medium. Reversibility: high. Consequences The session-start hook's prose sends every request in the session, questions and debugging included, to /booping:playbook project-manager instead of offering a playbook. OI-0036 must deliver: the project-manager gains a route \"a narrow playbook fits → run it\" and launches and orchestrates every other playbook; it escalates a conflict it sees in a decision to the owner; it files open items for the tails a piece of work leaves behind; given \"I have an hour\", it walks the open items and the specs, collects the conflicts and surfaces the critical ones for the owner to answer. OI-0037: soldering the manager render and the playbook table into session start waits until the manager entry proves itself. \"Proves itself\" for OI-0037 is the owner's call after about three days of working with the manager entry, with no numeric measure; feedback during those days comes as corrections, and OI-0038 asks the owner on 2026-10-22 whether the entry works and whether to solder it in, unless the feedback has overturned the concept by then (value/0010). Projects carry different levels of responsibility, so the manager's behaviour differs per project (value/0009): a home pet project or a product-fit experiment, where breakage costs little and the agent writes as much as it can — the owner only stays aligned by looking at the ADRs and the corpus of records the decisions rest on, and corrects them; a project past proof of concept, where the owner works closer to the code and wants more control; and a production system handling money, attribution and payouts, where a loss is very costly and the owner reviews everything by eye. This is set per project through project-tier rules targeted at project-manager; no new mechanism is needed now (owner, 2026-10-19).","claude-booping:conflict/0001":"Conflict value/0007 has the agent carry a plan through to the end on its own unless force majeure stops it. value/0009 has the human able to review the system deeply, or give the agent more freedom, and take control back easily. Every stop for the human's review is a point where the plan does not run on its own; every step run without review is a point where the human gave up control. Resolution The run's mode settles it, in prose (adr/0036): the user says so, or booping run … --autonomous launches it (value/0009). Supervised (the default) stops at the runs' review gates and the user drives groom then develop; autonomous lets a manager groom and build in batches, auto-confirming gates and recording the defaults it chose, so what the agent did without review stays visible. A state that should not exist, such as uncommitted changes on a branch when a migration runs, is asked about in either mode.","claude-booping:conflict/0002":"Conflict value/0008 checks the cheap, compact artefact before the expensive detail is built on it, at the trade-off of more review checkpoints in a run. value/0007 has the agent carry a plan to the end on its own, and value/0012 spends tokens rationally and keeps a balance between simplicity and the quality of the result. Each added checkpoint or review step is another stop, more calls and more minutes per run. Resolution adr/0015: each core playbook stops for review once and takes other user decisions in-step; the old groom's four confirm points went, since gate round-trips were the playbook's remaining cost. adr/0035: a fresh review of the plan lifts quality but adds about 10 minutes a plan (4–5 at best) for a gain inside the plans' own spread, so groom runs no separate review step; the step stays available behind core.groom_playbook.cross_review_agent for a user who wants it.","claude-booping:idea/0001":"Idea Before grooming starts planning, a researcher scans old plans and retrospectives for anything matching the request and returns only a list of related files. Groom shows the list to the user as a checklist: read them for a stronger start, or skip them so old data does not rot the context. On consent, a stronger researcher reads the picked files and returns a summary — related patterns, issues the retros flagged. The source suggests haiku for the listing pass and sonnet or opus for the summary. Today groom intake renders a \"Latest Plans\" table (state, name, summary, path) from the plan frontmatter; retrospectives are not offered, and there is no listing pass, user checklist or summary pass. How to check [clarify] The source names no check. Proposed: take two or three past requests in this vault whose retros flagged an issue, run groom on each twice — once as today, once with the retros' summary handed in by hand — and compare whether the plan avoids the flagged issue and what the extra pass costs in tokens (booping session-stats).","claude-booping:idea/0002":"Idea When learn turns a retro finding into a change, it should prefer guidance that helps a step avoid the issue over adding a validator. Sub-agents are expensive and belong only in critical situations. The learn steps (extract-candidates, write) and the target list in playbooks/learn/_partials/_learn_targets.j2 carry no such preference today. How to check [clarify] The source names no check. Proposed: count, over the rules in vault/rule/ and specs/rule/ and the learn runs recorded in vault/retrospectives/, how many proposals were a validator or a sub-agent check rather than guidance; if there are none, the idea has nothing to fix.","claude-booping:idea/0003":"Idea The owner wants to try different variants of the groom playbook, with one goal: find a groom that runs faster without losing quality (owner, 2026-10-15). At the time a plan of 12-29 SP spent 6-12 minutes in groom on top of 17-43 minutes of develop, and small plans paid the same fixed groom cost as large ones. Variants to compare included skipping groom for small mechanical plans (straight to develop with a short brief), a lighter graph, and depth scaled to the task, which went on as idea/0009. A measurement (reference/0006) puts the playbook groom at 2.4× the wall time, 5.2× the main-thread API calls and 3.0× the output tokens of the old /groom skill. The measurement does not say which steps the extra cost came from. How to check Experiment (owner, 2026-10-16; protocol in booping-benchmarks groom/): a corpus of past requests from two other projects (2 small, 2 medium, 2 complex), each in an isolated worktree at the commit before its plan with a vault stripped of plans, retros, notes and reviews; variants V0 the current groom, V1 a bare \"write a plan\", V2 bare + plan template, V3 template + stages, V4 V3 + cross-review (codex, fable, three OpenRouter models via pi-developer); metrics wall time, tokens and calls from booping session-stats, plan quality by a blind rubric judge against the historical plan, and for the best two a develop run scored by bench-score; each groom prompt paragraph probed against an empty-context agent to cut what the model already knows. Results land in a reference record, the chosen shape in an ADR. Added 2026-10-16 (owner): D1 — the whole development as one claude -p run with no groom or develop playbooks, compared on time and outcome with the historical pipeline; every variant runs in two isolation modes — a clean vault, and a vault with the project's plans, retros and reviews dated before the task (realistic: past plans may guide the model); historical reference plans came from older booping versions, so fresh references are made with the current booping at the end and the judge checks against both. Corpus: 6 done tasks, mostly web/client work (booping-benchmarks groom/corpus/). Owner's targets: small tasks (10-15 SP) reach develop with little or no grooming; complex ones keep research quality and also get faster; tokens about the same or lower. Run each variant on the same set of requests on the same commit, and compare plan quality (the groom evals' rubric, the plan's later develop outcome: rework, failed attempts) and cost (booping session-stats: wall time, tokens, API calls); a variant wins when its cost is clearly lower and quality holds. booping-benchmarks holds the harness for such runs. Verdict 2026-10-17: the benchmark (booping-benchmarks groom/, reference/0007) picked one drafting pass on the plan template, research then draft, with no self-review step (adr/0030): about 8 minutes a plan against 13–23 for the old groom, at equal or better judged quality. A script cuts the drafted plan into milestone files (adr/0031), and groom runs no separate review step (adr/0035). The light path for small, already-decided requests and depth scaled to the task are not done yet; they went on as idea/0009.","claude-booping:idea/0004":"Idea Two small trims of content that does not earn its place: Business-goal logic. The source says it does not help and is AI-generated. It is still in the code: the feature task type's description in src/config.yaml (\"Needs business goal\"), docs/task_feature.md (no goal → no ready-for-dev) and the \"Business-goal acceptance\" item in docs/plan_templates/backend.md. Retro action table. Remove the Owner column and add \"When to do it?\" in docs/retrospective_template.md. Today the Owner column already takes \"Next sprint / Person / standing\", so part of the change is a rename. How to check [clarify] The source names no check. Proposed: read the last five done feature plans and the last five retros in this vault and note whether the goal section or the Owner cell changed anything a later step did; if neither did, trim both.","claude-booping:idea/0005":"Idea Booping is wired to Claude Code and is hard to configure and share. A web service would handle playbook management and execution, with skills as thin wrappers over it; better still, the service also provides a sandbox where the workers run. Named parts: artefacts live in a web database (vault, vault entry, and a plan as a configured entry type with a state machine and templates), behind a REST API: CRUD on /api/{vault}/ and /api/{vault}/{entry}/, GET/PUT on /api/{vault}/states/{entry}/{id}; render is an application that calls the service; a task-tracker driver (Linear, or the service itself) creates the request, mirrors clarifications and the plan, and starts develop when the user moves the task; a router model maps a request to a playbook and runs it; custom playbooks, seeded with the default sprint playbook. MVP is groom and develop; retro and lessons come as increments after it. The source leaves open where the state hooks are configured. How to check [clarify] The source names no check. Proposed: answer the source's open question first — where state hooks live when state is a PUT on the API — by sketching groom's awaiting-clarification round-trip through the API and a tracker driver on paper; if the hooks cannot stay in the playbook's states: block, the service changes the playbook contract, and the idea needs an ADR before any code.","claude-booping:idea/0006":"Idea A thin benchmark harness built from real merged PRs: each PR is a self-contained bug, small feature or refactor, re-created as a booping plan (or a request to groom) at the PR's parent commit and verified by the repo's tests. Selection: merged, 80-250 changed lines, touches real test files, a clear task from the title and body, from the last two years, on stacks that set up easily (npm test, pytest). The source lists ten — eslint, prettier, got, axios ×2, koa, flask ×2, typer, httpx — plus four spares: eight bugs, one feature, one refactor, so it covers grooming a bug, grooming a feature and developing a pre-made plan. Neither benchmark built since uses this set: claude-booping-bench runs cases with injected faults on fastify and typer fixtures, and booping-benchmarks scores one frozen develop sprint. How to check [clarify] The source names no check. Proposed: take one PR from the list (flask #5917), groom it from its title and body at the parent commit, develop the plan, and run the PR's own test changes against the result; if pass/fail separates a good run from a bad one, the set is a usable signal.","claude-booping:idea/0007":"Idea The user wants Claude Code to add an agent to booping's harness when they need one — either a native Claude Code agent or a CLI agent (a pi call or another external harness) that saves tokens. Choosing such an agent by good_for / bad_for and delegating to it already works (core.{name}_playbook.agents.<id>), and documentation/integrating-external-agents.md and the external-agents recipe describe the manual steps; no playbook or skill does the wiring for the user. How to check [clarify] The source names no check. Proposed: in a fresh session on a project vault, ask Claude \"add a codex worker to develop\" with no other help and check whether it produces a working proxy agent, the config entry and the targeted rule from the documentation alone; if it does, the idea is covered by the docs and is rejected.","claude-booping:idea/0008":"Idea A plan's decisions today are lines in a Decisions table. The owner proposed (2026-10-16) that groom drafts a full ADR for each real fork the work meets — context, the alternatives with their trade-offs, the choice — so the decision goes up into the specs in the shape every other decision has, and the owner reviews the fork where it belongs. In a supervised run groom offers the drafts for review; in an autonomous run it records them as proposed with its choice and moves on. This is taken up after the groom refactoring (idea/0003) picks the leading variant, not inside its benchmark. How to check [clarify] Proposed: on two corpus tasks with a real fork (s1 storage of the feed identity, c1 slice boundary), groom with ADR drafts and without; compare the owner's review time and how often the owner changes the choice at review versus after development.","claude-booping:idea/0009":"Idea A small, already-decided request should not pay for a full groom (owner, 2026-10-16). Either groom routes it to a light path (request → one-pass develop brief) or the one-pass groom (adr/0030) is shown to clear it in minutes. More broadly, groom picks how much work to do from the task's complexity: a simple task or a bug gets a minimal pass, and deeper research runs when the task is complex or when the user asks for it — directly (\"plan tests\") or indirectly (\"do deep research for this task\"). This is the follow-up left after the variant pick of idea/0003. How to check Test case: corpus s3-booping-specs-site-registries in booping-benchmarks groom/, a site change the orchestrator first meant to groom and the owner sent straight to development; compare the current groom and the light path on time and on the develop outcome.","claude-booping:reference/0001":"Facts Every role is a technical professional; people without a technical background are not the audience (value/0014 — Comfort for non-technical users). User — an experienced developer or tech lead who installed booping and runs the groom → develop → retro → learn loop on their own repo via /playbook. Comes for installation and setup, the quick-start loop, what each playbook does to their repo and asks of them, per-playbook status vocabularies, the sprint/SP model and where vault artefacts land. User depth: commands, playbook names, status names, vault paths and the shape of a plan; never internals (template pipeline, Jinja rendering, Python modules, state-machine YAML). Withheld: build-time architecture, config-merge mechanics beyond \"put a config.yaml in your vault\", eval and snapshot infrastructure, unreleased behaviour. Voice: second person, imperative, worked examples over description, one happy path first and variations after. Reads the README, the docs site and release notes. Advanced user — a user shaping booping to a specific codebase: tuning config, authoring templates and rules, writing their own playbooks, wiring in external agents. Comes for the three-level config merge and per-project config keys, plan and review templates, targeted rules, playbook authoring (graphs, states, hooks) and external-agent integration. Advanced-user depth: every user-facing config key, the full playbook manifest surface, query specs and template trees as features; never plugin internals (build pipeline, Python source layout, CI gates). Withheld: how the render pipeline is implemented, snapshot and eval harness internals, config keys the core reserves for itself. Voice: second person, reference style, key tables and manifest shapes over narrative, one minimal example per mechanism. Reads the docs site, the playbook-authoring playbook and the template files in their own vault. Contributor — someone changing the plugin itself: templates, playbooks, the booping CLI or the testing infrastructure, sending PRs. Comes for the render pipeline, src/config.yaml semantics, playbook runtime mechanics, the snapshot and eval workflows and the CI gates a change must pass. Contributor depth: full internals down to module and template names, with pointers to the source over restated literals — the schema is the truth and prose only orients. Withheld: speculative roadmap and unconverged design directions. Voice: terse, declarative, repo-relative paths, assumes a checkout and just. Reads the repo CLAUDE.md, documentation/, the docs/ fragments, src/config.yaml comments, the justfile, bin/booping --help and PR and commit history.","claude-booping:reference/0002":"Facts Project Vault — the per-project directory holding every run artefact (plans/, retrospectives/, codereviews/, _playbooks/, templates, sprints.md): ~/Claude/{project}/ by default, or repo-local via the marker's vault_path: key. Core level — the plugin repo itself: the shipped playbooks, agents, the /playbook skill and the src/config.yaml defaults. Global level — the machine-wide tier: <home_dir>/ (_playbooks/, _specs/, templates) and ~/.config/booping/config.yaml. Project level — the tier scoped to one attached repo: the Project Vault and its config.yaml. Levels — the discovery and config-merge ladder, always named \"the core, global and project levels\"; later levels win on merge, names are unique across levels on discovery. Specs tiers (adr/0001) use the config level names: core, global (<home_dir>/_specs/, named by the user's namespace), project. Playbook — a multi-step guided procedure: a directory with playbook.md, an optional playbook.yaml (graph: plus state machines) and one directory per step. Step — one unit of a playbook's graph: a directory holding prompt.md, optionally per-model bodies and an eval suite. Subgraph — a mapping node in graph:: its own dependencies, an inner graph of steps, an optional prose repeat:, one nesting level only. Inline — a delegation level: the runner performs the step itself in the driving context. Assisted — a delegation level: the runner performs the step but hands heavy reads to the config-named research agent for a bounded return. Detached — a delegation level: the step runs in its own sub-agent, declared by the step's detached: field. Plan — a groomed sprint's document and run artefact, plans/{slug}/index.md in the Project Vault. Retrospective artefact — the standalone retrospectives/{slug}.md the retro track advances, linked back from the covered plan's retro: key. Targeted rule — an accepted rule record whose brief is injected into exactly the playbooks, steps, agents or skills its targets: list names; an untargeted one injects nowhere. Marker — the repo's .booping file attaching it to a Project Vault: project_name:, optional vault_path: and the latest_migration: watermark. Migration watermark — the marker's latest_migration: key, the highest shipped migration id a Project Vault has applied. Run artefact — the frontmatter-carrying file a state machine advances: a plan's index.md, a retrospective artefact, a code review. Cancelled — a terminal run outcome, reachable from any non-terminal status of an active run and carrying its own exit hooks. Query spec — a config-declared mapping at any dotted path describing a frontmatter query over a markdown directory. Template tree — a config-declared file tree booping create writes at a destination, its names and contents rendered as Jinja templates. Spec set — the project specs tier records the docs playbook reads: the value records, reference/0001–0003 and the story records.","claude-booping:reference/0003":"Facts README.md — for an engineer evaluating the plugin from the GitHub page: what it is, whether it fits, how to install, what the loop looks like. Ceiling: what-and-why plus first install; command references, config keys and playbook mechanics belong on the site. Its Statuses section is hand-maintained over the per-playbook states: blocks. documentation/ (the MkDocs site) — for users (quick start, per-playbook commands, status vocabularies) and advanced users (config merge, templates, rules, playbook authoring, external agents). The deep public reference, exhaustive on behaviour and configuration; plugin internals (build pipeline, snapshots, evals) stay out. documentation/recipes/ — for advanced users and contributors: one blogpost-like, end-to-end guide per non-trivial setup, in the user's own voice, linked from the README. Content is dictated by the user, never derived from delivered work; a documentation sweep may review a recipe for staleness but never generates or extends one, and a missing recipe is never a documentation gap. Reference pages stay the authority on behaviour. docs/ (top-level fragments) — for agents mid-run following a lazy link, and contributors deciding where route-specific detail belongs. Ceiling: exactly what the linking step needs; anything a human would browse belongs on the site. CLAUDE.md — for agents and contributors opening a session on the repo: where things live, which command gates a change, which files are build artefacts. Ceiling: the map, not the data; restated config values, status lists or long procedures are drift. CHANGELOG.md — for users and advanced users deciding whether to upgrade. Keep a Changelog shape, one entry per release in the public voice of the release notes: behaviour changes, new config keys, migrations to run; internal refactors and CI churn stay out. The product-vision spec set — for Claude itself, read before writing any other surface so a change lands in the right place; a stale set mis-aims every surface downstream. Ceiling: the project's model of itself (vision, roles, features, surfaces); never how-to prose, run history or implementation detail. The surface is the project specs tier records the docs playbook reads: the value records, reference/0001–0003 and the story records. Not surfaces: playbook source (playbooks/** manifests, step prompts, partials, fixtures, scripts), the generated playbooks/*/_reports/*.md snapshots, the skills/ and agents/ thin shells, src/templates/**, migrations/*/migration.md, the seeded docs/plan_templates/ and docs/review_templates/, docs/images/, .claude/skills/release/SKILL.md, test fixture markdown, the site/ build output, and source comments — they are executed, generated or seeded, not read as documentation.","claude-booping:reference/0004":"Facts Note dated 2026-04-29 (file date). The task, the plan, who or what graded, and how many runs per model are not named in the source. Claude Opus 4.7 — 9.7/10 overall: plan adherence 10, correctness and edge cases 10, code quality 9.5, testing 10, performance and safety 9. Verdict: clean, compliant with the architecture, handles edge cases. Minimax 2.7 — 7.2/10 overall: plan adherence 7, correctness 8, code quality 5, testing 7, performance and safety 9. Verdict: fixes the context-dropping bug but with severe DRY violations and algorithmic logic inside Jinja2 templates. GLM 5.1 — 6.4/10 overall: plan adherence 9, correctness 4, code quality 6, testing 8, performance and safety 5. Verdict: fails to pass context into nested partials, which breaks the pipeline and adds heavy I/O.","claude-booping:reference/0005":"Facts 2026-07-09, case fastify-naming-lesson in claude-booping-bench: /develop followed the casing in the plan's code sketch over a loaded lesson that named a different convention, in 3 of 3 reps; judgment score 0.2. Bench runs used booping's native agents only; pi-developer was deregistered from the vault config on 2026-07-09. The booping version and model under test are not named in the source; the run was then the skill-based /develop, before the develop playbook. How to check In a local clone of the bench harness: uv run booping-bench run --case fastify-naming-lesson --reps 3, then uv run booping-bench report <run-id>.","claude-booping:reference/0006":"Facts Note dated 2026-08-02 (file date). The request groomed, the model and whether both runs used the same request are not named in the source. Wall time (span): 9m 13s old /groom vs 21m 53s playbook groom (2.4×); active time 5m 53s vs 16m 11s (2.75×); user wait 3m 20s vs 5m 42s. Main-thread API calls: 20 vs 104 (5.2×). Output tokens (main): 47,621 vs 144,317 (3.0×). Cache reads 1.32M vs 10.24M (7.7×); cache writes 186k vs 573k (3.1×). Final context ~88k vs ~161k (1.8×). Tool calls: 8 (3 Read, 2 AskUserQuestion, 1 Write, 1 Bash, 1 Agent) vs 45 (21 Edit, 15 Bash, 4 Write, 3 Read, 2 Agent). Researcher agents: 1 (63k tokens, 60s, 11 tools) vs 2 (89k + 66k tokens, 137s + 113s, 29 + 29 tools); agent time ~1m vs ~4m 10s. User interaction: 2 AskUserQuestion and no typed replies vs no AskUserQuestion and 2 typed prose replies. How to check [clarify] The source names no recipe; booping session-stats on the two groom sessions gives the token figures.","claude-booping:reference/0007":"Facts Data: GitLab lab/booping-benchmarks, branch feat/groom-bench, directory groom/ — corpus (corpus/<task>/task.yaml, incidents.yaml), runs (results/r1, results/r2, results/r2-redo), judges (results/*/judge*), ablation/report.md, split/report.md, xreview/. Client feed inputs are not in git. Corpus: six past tasks (s1, s2 small; m1, m2 medium; c1, c2 complex), plus routing cases s3 (a small decided request sent to develop without a groom) and m3 (a medium request groomed by the old groom). Judging: free judges with no rubric and no final solution (codex, a neutral claude -p, Gemini 3.1 Pro through pi with code access); a mechanical incidents metric — share of what really went wrong that a plan would have prevented (major ×2, minor ×1); c1's incidents are not plan-preventable. Variants, round 2 (12 plans per task on s1, m2, c1): staged draft V3 6.7 and V3a 6.6 mean judge score, template-only V2 5.8, V3 plus five old groom rules V5 5.3; the old multi-step groom V0 took 13–23 min and $4.25–5.62 a plan against V3a's 7.9 min and $2.75. Only staged variants found m2's 60 s gateway take cap. Spread inside one variant (V3: 9 and 4 on s1) exceeds the V3–V3a gap. Rule ablation (45 rules of V3 in 9 batches, 40 runs, s1 and s2): 16 rules the model follows without being told, 16 carried by the template's sections, 9 milestone-shape rules it misses even when told (36–58%), 4 weak; removing a batch does not change the cost per plan ($2.16–2.73 against $2.33). Telling the model more can hurt: V5's five extra rules made it the fastest and the worst variant and it never found the m2 defect. Plan split (12 single-file plans): a script cutting on headings passes every threshold on 12/12 at $0; Sonnet rewriting 12/12 at $0.71; Haiku mapping line ranges 8/12; Haiku rewriting 6/12. Review as a separate step (groom/xreview, 36 plans, Opus fold-in, judged on readiness and on challenging the request): a fresh Opus \"double-check\" helps (readiness +1.77 / +1.22, challenge +2.27 / +1.42, codex / claude-p) and beats V3's in-session self-review head to head, at +10.4 min a plan; the cross-review prompt on Opus, codex and Gemini show no measurable effect; no reviewer found m2's 60 s cap. Decision: adr/0035 (no review step). Cost of the Claude runs recorded in result.json: variants $145 (61 runs), ablation $97 (40), split $16 (48) — about $257 and 302 M tokens, mostly cache reads; codex and Gemini judges, the claude -p judges and the implementation runs are not in that sum. How to check jq -s '[.[] | .total_cost_usd // 0] | add' $(find groom -name result.json) in the benchmark repo. groom/ablation/rule_table.md and groom/split/report.md hold the per-rule and per-plan numbers.","claude-booping:reference/0008":"Facts 2026-10-15 07:14–07:18 UTC, orchestrator session 36dbf90d (another project): the owner asked to fold orchestrate and backlog-run into one core playbook; usage limits were not mentioned. ~07:21: a research subagent listed backlog-run's budget gate as one of its unique mechanics and recommended carrying budget and window gates into autonomous mode. The agent passed it to the owner as one line in prose — «autonomous: гейт бюджета, ночное окно, пачки по размеру» — and asked whether to keep the nightly timer; the question was never answered. 08:12: the owner approved the package («Ну вроде ок все, давай планируй»); 30 s later adr/0028 was committed with \"carries over backlog-run's budget and window gates, pause and resume\", under \"The owner settled the shape\". 13:49: the orchestrator agent, not the owner, wrote the request of plan 202610152053_project-manager-autonomous-extras; groom and develop ran autonomously, the plan was approved by the run itself (\"the owner was unavailable\"), with no code review and no retro. The values the plan cited (value/0015, value/0009, value/0007, value/0005, value/0006) justified how the gate was built, never whether booping should have one; no value then marked a scope boundary. 2026-10-17 the owner ruled usage limits out of scope (adr/0028, value/0017); removal is OI-0004, done on 2026-10-18. On 2026-10-18 adr/0028 itself, a decision with no real alternative, gave way to value/0019 and story/0023. Causes: the agent's own proposal was buried in prose instead of a list the owner could accept line by line; an unanswered question counted as a yes; adr/0028 attributed agent proposals to the owner, against rule/0002; nothing in the values said what booping does not do. Remedies: booping:rule/0033 (decisions listed for acceptance), value/0017 and the value/0006 / value/0018 split (scope boundary). How to check Transcript of session 36dbf90d-0102-4f43-add1-4a69775c2e2f (private, not published) around 2026-10-15T07:14–08:20Z. The request and clarifications of plan 202610152053_project-manager-autonomous-extras (private run history, not published); commit 1e4dfd6 (adr/0028).","claude-booping:reference/0009":"Facts Setup: claude -p --model claude-opus-5-5 --setting-sources \"\" --tools \"\", no booping context beyond a fixed description of the driver, the vault and the CLI's subcommands. Scenarios and their expected answers were written by the agent after the owner's decisions of the day (adr/0036, adr/0037), so they are a signal, not a proof; 2–3 repeats a cell. Probe 1 — which wording decides prose (A), an extended existing primitive (B) or a new CLI or config key (C). Eleven scenarios: autonomy mode (expected A), an agent-decisions log with a corrections CLI already present (A/B), item questions read by status (B), idea capture (A/B), and controls — transitions, a run registry, plan split, a status report, entry timestamps and session id, a render-time slice of open entries (B/C), milestone feedback (A). Wording Right Autonomy Feedback none 14/16 C C A A value/0015 + rule/0025 + rule/0030 as of 2026-10-17 12/16 C C B B \"a mechanism earns its place\" 14/16 C C A A ladder: prose → extend existing → new 14/16 C B A A old texts + ladder 14/16 B B B B old texts + \"fewest mechanisms\" value 13/16 C C A B \"who reads the result\" 16/16, and 33/33 on all eleven × 3 A A A A old texts + \"who reads the result\" 15/16 C C A A \"who reads\" + examples + \"when unsure, prose\" 31/33 C A C A A A the above + \"count only readers that exist or are asked for\" (rule/0025 now) 33/33 A A A A A A The old wording pushed a prose task into code: \"only the findings text needs judgement; capturing, persisting and handing over are computable → code\" (milestone feedback as a validated transition payload). Beside the old texts the new wording loses the autonomy case: the old texts must be replaced, not joined. Adding \"a prompt filled in at render time\" as a code reader let the model justify a config key by the template branch it would itself add — the shape core.autonomy had. The \"readers that exist or are asked for\" clause closed it. Probe 2 — groom an item with a sibling of the same shape. Pairs: corrections → agent decisions, flaky-test register → rejected review findings; control: run registry → per-plan cost. Brief Same-shape pairs (6 runs) Control (3 runs) the item alone no new CLI: open items with a kind, query at render, items close extends session-stats + the sibling's groomed plan (which adds a CLI) 6/6 generalise the sibling's CLI into one (ledger, register) 3/3 reuse, no forced merge + \"same batch, similar shape\" from the manager same as above 2 reuse, 1 separate Probe 3 — both items groomed together in one plan. Same pairs: 6/6 one mechanism for both, 5/6 with no new CLI (open items with a kind, or a note per entry through scaffold, frontmatter-update and a render-time query); control 3/3 one design (cost rolled up from run records), sensible rather than forced. A sibling's plan anchors the next one: it generalises the sibling's new CLI instead of returning to an existing primitive. Grooming together sees the shared shape first and does not anchor. Parallel grooming without the sibling is how the corrections, defaults and questions CLIs came to exist side by side (plans 202610151532 and 202610151609, groomed the same afternoon). How to check Prompts, variants and raw answers: ~/.tmp/booping-probes-2026-10-18/exp (probe 1, run1.sh, sc/, var/, out/) and exp2 (probes 2 and 3, run1.sh, runT.sh, out/). Re-run one cell: ./run1.sh <scenario> <variant> <rep> from that directory.","claude-booping:reference/0010":"Facts 2026-10-15: one owner request was cut into sibling plans groomed the same afternoon — corrections log (202610151532), autonomy modes (202610151609), then the project manager. Each built its own mechanism: booping correction add|list with a transcript lookup proving the quote verbatim and a dialogue link; core.autonomy with an invalid-value STOP, a project-context line and a render branch; booping defaults add; booping items ask|answer with a two-part answered test. About 550 lines and 35 e2e cases. core.autonomy never decided anything booping run --autonomous did not: a supervised project's child run already got the supervised text plus the autonomous preamble, and the preamble won. adr/0026 weighed \"log as it happens\" against \"mine transcripts at retro\"; adr/0027 weighed \"mode per project\" against \"one fixed mode\". Neither weighed prose, an existing primitive or building nothing. An audit of all ADRs the same day scored 6 of 32 as checkbox alternatives (0016, 0017, 0019, 0026, 0027, and the first draft of 0037) and 18 as missing one cheap option; every 1 introduced a mechanism, and authorship did not matter — the owner's one mechanism ADR scored 1 too. 2026-10-18 the owner asked why core.autonomy was not a boolean, then why it existed, then what correction add bought over the model writing the file. The agent first reconstructed reasons for both designs, then reversed within the hour. adr/0036 moved autonomy to prose; adr/0037 replaced the three CLIs with vault files over scaffold, frontmatter-update and query, after a generic booping journal was built and discarded the same day. Probes the same day (reference/0009): the old wording of value/0015 and rule/0025 (\"whatever can be computed runs as code\") scored worst of all variants, below no rule at all, and moved a prose task into a validated transition payload; a fresh model given a sibling's plan generalised that plan's new CLI, and given the items alone or together it built none. Causes: Code had no bar to clear. rule/0030 asks a prompt instruction for a measured miss; rule/0025 sent to code whatever could be computed, with no reader asked for. A value pulling toward code had no counterweight, so a plan citing value/0015 never had a departure to flag — the same gap reference/0008 found: values justified how a mechanism was built, never whether it should exist. A mechanism justified itself: the config key was read by the template branch made for it. The ADR guide asked for a real alternative but named none of the cheap ones, and its \"'leave it as it is' … is no option\" read as leave to drop building nothing; rows: {min: 2} passes \"new vs old\". Siblings of one class were groomed in parallel, each blind to the other; when one sees the other's plan, it anchors on it. The agent defended existing code by reconstruction before testing what it bought. Remedies: rule/0025 rewritten: who reads the result decides prose or code, readers counted only when they exist or are asked for, prose when unsure; value/0015 rewritten to match. Wording chosen by probe. src/specs/README.md: a decision that adds a mechanism weighs prose, an existing primitive and building nothing, each as a row or with one line saying why it does not apply. booping:rule/0035: the manager grooms same-class items together or in sequence with the earlier plan linked, never in parallel. Agent memory: on \"why is X so\", records first, then \"what do we lose without X\". OI-0006: run the loss test over every booping subcommand. How to check adr/0026, adr/0036, adr/0037; git show 3db6f75:vault/adr/0027-autonomy-modes.md; commit 2597086. Plans vault/plans/202610151532_corrections-log/, vault/plans/202610151609_autonomy-modes/. reference/0009 and its raw data in ~/.tmp/booping-probes-2026-10-18/.","claude-booping:reference/0011":"Facts 2026-10-17/18: booping budget was removed in d5670a0 and booping correction and booping defaults in 50f8495, on release/2.0.0. At the same time another project ran autonomous booping runs, and their playbooks and rules still called these commands: project-manager's \"Corrections\" used booping correction add, and autonomous gates logged via booping defaults add. The runs failed with invalid choice: 'budget' (that project's feedback, point 6). The current tree holds no reference to the removed commands (checked 2026-10-18). booping on PATH is this repo's working tree, so runs in every other project execute whatever the release branch holds at that moment (2026-10-18). The operator rename in plan 202610180333_vault-file-operators hits live runs the same way. Cause: a removal landed in the working tree that live runs in other projects execute from. Nothing tells a live run that the CLI it calls changed under it. Remedy: [clarify] not decided. Candidates are to run other projects only on a released plugin version, or to have a removal leave a stub that prints what replaced the command. Owner to settle. How to check The other project's feedback, point 6 (private note, not published); commits d5670a0 and 50f8495.","claude-booping:rule/0002":"Rule A record or a run artefact states only what its source says: the conversation, the user's answers, the code, a measurement, a document read. An option, an argument or a detail the source lacks is written as \"not named in the source\"; a choice the agent made by default is marked [clarify] with one sentence on what to settle; a reason that is only the user's preference is called that. A record drafted from a dialogue states as decided only what the user accepted on the decision list (booping:rule/0033); the rest is [clarify]. Follows from Adjustable control — the human finds what the agent decided alone only if it is marked. Effective human review — a review checks the marks instead of rereading everything for what was guessed.","claude-booping:rule/0003":"Rule A run with a human review step first produces a compact artefact — a feature index before the stories, a plan outline before the milestones — and stops for the human to read, reorder, fix and decompose it. The expensive detail is built only on the reviewed artefact, so a later review never sends the run several steps back. Follows from Effective human review — the cheap artefact is where a wrong direction is cheapest to catch.","claude-booping:rule/0005":"Rule A playbook run that hands control back to the user ends by naming the next step and the exact way to call it — after grooming, how to call develop. A call on an artefact that is not ready for it — an under-groomed plan given to develop — says what is missing and which step makes it ready. The user may take another path; the hint does not block it. Follows from Guides to the goal — the system leads to the goal and lets a professional step aside.","claude-booping:rule/0012":"Rule Retired on 2026-10-14: the owner deactivated the Plannotator review for now and may revisit it. While it was accepted: this project reviews every diff in Plannotator; the browser review replaces presenting findings in chat. Once findings are computed, they are handed to the plannotator-reviewer agent with the diff ref (a PR URL, or a token such as HEAD for the working tree), the repo path, and every finding as file:lineStart[-lineEnd] · side · severity · snippet · proposed-fix · rationale. The review is launched even with zero findings, so the human sees the clean diff. The agent's reply is the human's feedback, and the run continues from it. If the agent is not among the available agents, the run stops and tells the user to install ~/.claude/agents/plannotator-reviewer.md and register it in the vault config, then restart Claude Code. If the agent returns SURFACE UNAVAILABLE, the run reports exactly what failed and stops; it never substitutes a chat review. Follows from Effective human review — the human reviews the diff inline with the findings, where it is convenient for them; the owner's preference for this project. _lessons/0012_review_every_diff_in_plannotator.md (removed by migration 004) — the source names no retro.","claude-booping:rule/0013":"Rule The committed playbook reports (playbooks/*/_reports/output.md) are the user's review gate over playbook sources. Neither the orchestrator nor a worker runs just snapshots-accept or writes a report. When a sprint touches playbook sources (manifests, step prompt.md, shared partials, src/templates/), wrap-up runs the read-only just snapshots and does not stop on drift: the run commits the sources, leaves the reports as they are, and its final report names each drifted playbook with the just snapshots-accept {playbook} command. The user reviews and accepts the drift later, in a commit of their own. Until then just ci fails at its snapshots step, so a run's CI gate treats a failure that is snapshot drift alone as passed and reports it; any other failure still fails the gate (owner, 2026-10-17; develop's only stop stays the branch, adr/0015). No drift, nothing to report. Follows from Adjustable control — a change to what playbooks say stays visible to the human instead of passing the gate silently. Proven to work — the baseline holds the rendered behaviour; accepting it from inside a run empties the check. Plans run to the end — drift is reported, never a stop.","claude-booping:rule/0015":"Rule A code review is scoped to code files. Documentation, plans and other prose in the diff are out of scope and get no findings — a diff touching src/ modules, a plan's index.md and a README gets findings on the src/ files only. The reason is not named in the source; it is the owner's preference. Follows from _lessons/0015_review-only-code-files.md (removed by migration 004) — the source names no retro.","claude-booping:rule/0017":"Rule Structured data — statuses, queues, agent ids, plan and milestone globs, table columns — lives in src/config.yaml (or a tier above it) and in query specs, and reaches a skill or step body by rendering. Body prose names the source and never restates the literal value, because a copied literal decays while the schema does not. Follows from Single source — config is the source; prose never restates it.","claude-booping:rule/0018":"Rule Core skills and playbooks name no stack and no project. A project's specifics — its conventions, commands, the shaping of an agent for it — live in the project's own destinations: rule records in the project specs tier and the attached repo's CLAUDE.md, the only two places learn writes (booping:rule/0001). A run never writes a project specific into the core or into a user-level scope such as ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. Follows from Stack-agnostic core — specifics live in the global and the project's tiers.","claude-booping:rule/0019":"Rule The driver of a playbook run owns every vault read and write, the run state and the repo commits. A worker agent, of any harness, touches only the repo's code — or, after the user explicitly confirms it for the plan, code outside the repo: it runs its milestone's Verify and leaves the work uncommitted, never touching git history. The other way round, the driver does not patch code itself — a failing check is handed to a worker to fix. Follows from v1.0.1 changelog — when the worker committed, a commit that failed the DoD check could already be on the branch, and a worker driven through a foreign harness had to handle git. value/0002 — a worker gets only what it needs. In the 2026-04-23 and 2026-04-22 retros agent bodies picked up orchestrator concepts (lessons, plan statuses) they did not need, and the orchestrator bypassed the CLI write path for plan files; the split keeps both out. value/0003 — each fact, decision and piece of structured data lives in one place and is referenced or rendered from there. Development quality checks — a quality failure is delegated to a worker, not patched in the orchestrator. Exceptions A small fix goes to the session that already holds its whole context instead of a fresh worker: at most core.sprint.inline_fix_sp SP on the sprint scale (core.sprint.scale), every file it touches already read in that session, nothing left to find out — no file still to read, no research, no open question (owner, 2026-10-17). Anything larger, or with an unknown, goes to a worker. develop — the driver applies such a fix itself. code-review — a finding accepted at the verdict is applied by the reviewer that found it, resumed by its id, never respawned; when it cannot be resumed, the fix goes to a worker, not to the driver. Accepted at the verdict means by the user in a supervised run, or settled by the driver in an autonomous one and logged as an agent decision in agent-decisions/. project-manager — no exception: its driver delegates every code change, keeping its long-lived context clean. In an autonomous run, the driver commits such fixes separately from the workers' work, and they pass the same check as a worker's fix; a red check reverts the fix and hands it to a worker.","claude-booping:rule/0020":"Rule When a project needs a core agent or skill to behave differently, it writes a record targeted at it — agent:<id> or skill:<name> — which is injected into that agent's or skill's body. It does not add a separate extension file beside the agent or skill. The record is a rule with targets: (booping:rule/0001). Follows from Single source — one record per adjustment, no second extension mechanism restating it. Stack-agnostic core — the core agent stays generic; the project tier adds.","claude-booping:rule/0022":"Rule Every commit message follows Conventional Commits with a scope: feat(booping): …, fix(groom): …. The reason is not named in the source; it is the owner's convention. Follows from CLAUDE.md, Editing conventions — the source states the convention without a reason.","claude-booping:rule/0023":"Rule Each playbook's states: block is the authority on its statuses. The README's Statuses section is a hand-maintained narrative over them, not rendered from them, so a change to a states: block revisits that section. [clarify] The source says \"revisit\"; whether it must land in the same commit is not named in the source. Follows from Single source — the narrative is a known copy, kept honest by hand until it is rendered.","claude-booping:rule/0024":"Rule A new procedure is written as a playbook that the one shipped skill, /playbook, drives. A new skill is added only for a surface /playbook cannot drive. Follows from adr/0002 — every procedure ships as a playbook driven by the single /playbook skill, carrying run state and loading step bodies one at a time. value/0002 — a step gets only what it needs. value/0005 — the core stays stack-agnostic; specifics live in the global and the project tiers.","claude-booping:rule/0025":"Rule Who reads the result decides where it is produced. Code reads it, or it is a fact outside the model's view → a CLI, extending or generalising an existing one first. Code reads it: a guard, a status report, another command, a slice of entries filled into a prompt at render time. Facts outside the model's view: the current time, the session id, a process, a transcript, many files at once. Only a model or a human reads it — instructions, a mode of behaviour, findings, a note someone reviews → prose, with a format doc if the shape matters. Count only readers that exist today or that the request names. Code the proposed design would add to read the result does not count: a config key read only by the template branch it was made for justifies neither. Exact transforms the model was measured to get wrong are the one exception (adr/0031). When unsure, choose prose with a fixed format. A CLI over that format is a later optimisation, taken when code starts to read it. What code produces is trusted: a transition's printed mutation report is authoritative, and the runner does not re-read frontmatter to verify it. Follows from Deterministic where code reads it — code produces what code reads; prose holds the rest. Quality at a rational cost — every command and key is code to keep; a mechanism without a reader is cost without gain. Prose or code probes — the wording that held on every probe, and the old \"whatever can be computed\" wording that pushed prose tasks into code.","claude-booping:rule/0026":"Rule Every step body is fetched on demand by whoever runs the step; it is never pasted into a spawn prompt. A run's context stays proportional to its graph, not to the sum of its step bodies. Follows from Minimal effective context — detail is read on demand, never inlined in case it helps.","claude-booping:rule/0027":"Rule A hermetic render repeats byte for byte on any machine: macro stubs pin every config-declared macro, and --project pins the config merge to the core and project levels, so nothing from the machine or the global level leaks in. A change that makes a render depend on anything else breaks this rule. Follows from Proven to work — the committed snapshot reports can only hold behaviour if the render is reproducible.","claude-booping:rule/0028":"Rule Every project-vault artefact is plain markdown with YAML frontmatter — no other format and no database — so the vault opens as is in Obsidian. Follows from Everything in one place — everything is plain files, easy to look at from one place. Analysable history — a stable plain shape stays comparable across projects.","claude-booping:rule/0029":"Rule A problem in a playbook graph or a render surfaces in the rendered output as a **STOP — tell the user:** or **Note — tell the user:** notice, with exit 0. It never surfaces as a crash. Follows from Guides to the goal — the user is told what is wrong inside the run instead of facing a stack trace.","claude-booping:rule/0030":"Rule An instruction goes into a playbook prompt, a partial or a targeted rule only when the model is shown getting that thing wrong without it. The evidence is the smallest that settles it, like a unit test: one input, the prompt, one model call, and a look at the answer — or a correction already recorded. A larger eval suite or a benchmark costs hours and money and runs only when the owner agrees to it; propose it with its cost and what it would settle (owner, 2026-10-16). What the model does unprompted stays out of the prompt. An instruction the model misses even when told moves out of prose into a deterministic check or a structural step. Removing instructions is checked the same way, smallest evidence first. Follows from Minimal effective context — 16 of 45 groom rules were instructions the model follows anyway. Quality at a rational cost — five extra rules made the groom variant cheaper and worse (reference/0007). Proven to work — an instruction is kept for a measured reason, not for how plausible it sounds. Exceptions Owner decisions and safety constraints (secrets, irreversible actions) are stated without waiting for a miss.","claude-booping:rule/0031":"Rule A step, an internal agent, a fallback or an eval config whose work needs judgement — drafting, reviewing, folding findings in, verifying a Definition of Done, consulting the specs, writing a manifest or a test suite — runs on Opus. Sonnet and Haiku are used only for a mechanical step whose expected output is known in advance: classifying against a fixed list, extracting fields, writing a one-line record. A cheaper model is not benchmarked as a candidate for judgement work. The researcher (agents/booping-researcher.md) runs on Sonnet with high effort: its job is to find, read and summarise material for an Opus caller that keeps the judgement, and Opus on high is overkill for it (owner, 2026-10-17). Follows from Quality at a rational cost — booping is a professional tool and runs on a subscription; a lazy result costs more time than the model saves (owner, 2026-10-17: Sonnet chose to do almost nothing in the owner's own agent game, Opus performs well). Proven to work — the playbooks are measured on Opus; a step on another model is unmeasured. Exceptions A user's own config may pick any model for any step.","claude-booping:rule/0032":"Rule A command in the booping CLI is a generic primitive any playbook could use — render, query, create, set, transition, state, config. Behaviour that belongs to one playbook's domain, such as cutting a groom draft into milestone files, is a script in that playbook's _scripts/ (or the shared playbooks/_scripts/ when several core playbooks use it) and runs through the playbook's hooks, the same mechanism a user's own playbook gets (owner, 2026-10-18). Follows from A framework for playbooks — the CLI is the framework's primitive set, not a home for one procedure. A core set for development and self-learning — a core playbook uses only mechanics a user playbook gets too.","claude-booping:rule/0033":"Rule github.com/A/claude-booping is public, so every tracked file is published on push. Another project's data — its name tied to its numbers, its plan or task text, its findings — local absolute paths, and raw experiment inputs are written only under vault/notes/, which is gitignored; references go to vault/notes/references/. A tracked record keeps aggregates only (for example \"92 plans across 7 projects\", a range instead of a per-project list) and names such a source as \"(private note, not published)\" without linking it. Before committing a vault path, grep the staged diff for other projects' names, /tmp/ and /home/, and move what matches (owner, 2026-10-18, after a feedback note with local paths, another project's raw task text and a per-project breakdown were moved into a tracked directory and nearly pushed). Follows from Everything in one place — the private sources stay in the same vault, beside the records that cite them, just out of git. Exceptions A project name alone, without its data, may appear where the owner already published it.","claude-booping:runbook/0001":"When User-visible changes have landed on master and should reach users, who pick them up with /plugin update booping. Before you start master is up to date with the GitHub remote, which is named gh, not origin. just ci is green on master. The version follows semver: a patch for fixes only, no new feature and no config change. Steps Cut release/X.Y.Z from master. Set \"version\" in .claude-plugin/plugin.json to X.Y.Z; marketplace.json carries no version. Write the ## vX.Y.Z — YYYY-MM-DD entry at the top of CHANGELOG.md: one lead line, then ### Added / ### Changed / ### Fixed bullets of user-visible effects in the past tense. The docs user playbook's changelog step writes the same shape when it has run since the last release; earlier entries are never reworded. Commit both files as release: vX.Y.Z. Push the branch to gh and open a PR into master titled release: vX.Y.Z; its body names the base commit, the two files changed, the PRs included and why the bump is patch, minor or major. Merge the PR with a merge commit. Create the GitHub release vX.Y.Z on master (gh release create vX.Y.Z --target master), which tags the merge commit. The notes open with the changelog's lead line and give each change its own ## section, in more depth than the changelog. Check gh release list shows vX.Y.Z as Latest, and git tag has vX.Y.Z on the merge commit after a fetch. .claude-plugin/plugin.json on master reads X.Y.Z. The docs workflow ran green on the push to master (it publishes the site to gh-pages). Rollback No release has been rolled back and there is no rollback procedure; the owner finds that acceptable for now (2026-10-14). A broken release is fixed by the next patch release.","claude-booping:runbook/0002":"When A change touches a playbook step that has an eval suite (promptfooconfig.yaml beside its prompt.md), or a PR wants evidence of how the steps behave. CI never runs evals. Before you start Claude Code is logged in on a subscription: the provider runs claude -p and strips ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from its environment so nothing is billed to an API key. npx and gh are on the path, and the branch has an open PR if the result should be posted; without one the report prints to stdout. Steps List the suites with just suites. Run the deterministic tier first: just smoke <playbook>/<step>, or just smoke all. It makes no judge calls. Run the judged tier when the smoke tier is clean: just regress <playbook>/<step>. It costs judge calls; just eval runs both tiers, just eval-md also renders a local markdown report. To keep a run off the PR, prefix it with EVAL_PR=0. Read the PR comment: one fenced block whose header names the tier, branch, head SHA and recipe, then the tally, then one section per suite — failing suites first — with a PASS/FAIL line per case and each failing check nested under it, named by its MDCHECK: / FILES: / RETURN: tag or assertion type. Treat a judged-tier failure as a lead, not a verdict: the suites judge freshly generated artefacts, so a full run lands on a different red set each time. Re-run the one suite before acting on a single red case. Check The PR has one comment starting with <!-- booping-eval -->, and its <!-- sha: … --> line matches the head commit; a later push makes it stale, and a re-run edits the same comment in place. [unknown] scripts/eval-pr-comment.sh says an evals CI gate greps the marker line, but .github/workflows/ci.yml has no eval job and the justfile calls the comment advisory; which is meant is not confirmed.","claude-booping:runbook/0003":"When just snapshots reports drift after an edit to playbook sources (manifests, step prompt.md, shared partials, src/templates/), or a txtar case in just e2e fails after an intended change in CLI output. Before you start The baselines are the user's review gate: an agent run or a worker briefing never runs just snapshots-accept, --txtar-update, or writes playbooks/*/_reports/output.md. At the end of a sprint it shows the drift and asks the user to accept. Steps Run just snapshots and read the whole diff; it writes nothing. If any hunk is not what the source edit meant, fix the source and repeat step 1. Accept the drift for the playbooks you read: just snapshots-accept <playbook>, or just snapshots-accept for all. For a txtar contract case, run just e2e -k <case> --txtar-update, then read the rewritten case file in git diff the same way. Commit the source and its new baseline together, in one commit. Check just snapshots and just e2e pass. just mdcheck passes over the accepted reports. git diff --stat HEAD~1 shows the source edit and its _reports/output.md or txtar case in the same commit. Rollback Restore the previous baseline with git checkout HEAD~1 -- <path> if it was committed by mistake, and return to step 1.","claude-booping:story/0001":"As a user, I want one /playbook skill to find a procedure and drive its steps in graph order, so that any procedure runs to completion without me sequencing it. Scenarios The user invokes /playbook and sees the playbooks visible from the core, global and project levels. The user names a playbook, or describes the work and the skill selects the playbook whose trigger matches. The skill renders the playbook's procedure and drives its steps in graph order: a runner-performed step's body is fetched with booping render-playbook --step when the step runs, a detached step goes to its sub-agent, which fetches its own body. After each batch of steps the run pauses at their review gates, presents the output, and continues on the user's explicit confirmation, until the procedure is complete. Resuming: the user invokes the same playbook on a run that was interrupted; the skill reads the state frontier and continues from the first unfinished step. Edge cases Two levels hold a playbook of the same name: the clash is flagged and the run is blocked. The user stays silent at a review gate: the run waits, it never passes the gate on silence. An assisted step loops back: the follow-up goes to the same research agent by id, not to a fresh one. A graph or render problem: the run surfaces it as a STOP or Note notice to tell the user, not as a crash. Criteria Lists the playbooks visible from the core, global and project levels and selects one by its trigger or by name; a name clash across levels is flagged and blocks the run. Drives steps in graph order, each parallel wave as detached sub-agents, with one-level subgraphs. Delegates each step inline, assisted or detached per its detached: field; an assisted step hands heavy reads to the config-named research agent, resumed by id across a loopback. Loads step bodies lazily with booping render-playbook --step. Pauses at review gates for an explicit verdict, never running past one on silence, and resumes an interrupted run from the state frontier.","claude-booping:story/0002":"As an advanced user, I want run state held in declared states: machines that only the CLI advances, so that a run's status is never re-derived or hand-edited by the model. Scenarios A playbook author declares a states: machine in playbook.yaml: the artefact, the initial status, the statuses and their transitions with when, gates and hooks. A run reaches a step outcome; the driver matches it against a transition's when, judges its gates, and moves the artefact with the CLI. The CLI writes the new status and runs the transition's hooks — frontmatter stamps and script hooks — and prints a mutation report the driver takes as authoritative. Later, the driver asks the CLI for the run's state and gets every artefact's status and the per-instance frontier, from which an interrupted run resumes. Edge cases A machine declares no artifact:: the artefact is addressed with --target. The user cancels a run in any non-terminal status: it moves to cancelled and that edge's own exit hooks run. A script hook's name exists both in the playbook's _scripts/ and in a shared _scripts/ root: the playbook's own wins, then the most specific discovery root. --append on a list key that is absent creates it, and adds a value already present only once. Criteria booping playbook-transition is the sole writer of run state, guarded by when conditions and gates declared in playbook.yaml. frontmatter-update hooks stamp any workdir-relative file with {instance} interpolation, and --append adds to a list key, creating and de-duplicating it. script hooks resolve playbook-dir first, then by discovery-root specificity over the shared _scripts/ roots, passing argv through. cancelled is a terminal outcome reachable from any non-terminal status, with its own exit hooks; --target addresses a run artefact when the machine declares no artifact:. booping playbook-state reports every run artefact's status and a per-instance frontier, so per-milestone statuses resume a run.","claude-booping:story/0003":"As an advanced user, I want to override any config key at the global or project level, so that booping fits my machine and my codebase without touching the plugin. Scenarios The user sets a key in ~/.config/booping/config.yaml, and it applies to every project on the machine. The user sets the same key in the Project Vault's config.yaml, and the project's value wins for that project. The user reads the merged value back with the CLI to see what a run will use. The user declares a query spec or a template tree at a config path, then runs it with booping query --config or writes it with booping create, each write printed as a unified diff. Edge cases A list is overridden at a later level: it replaces the earlier list wholesale, it is not merged. booping create meets an existing target: it skips it unless --force. A write leaves a file unchanged: nothing is printed. A render needs to be reproducible: the macros are stubbed instead of run. Criteria Any key can be overridden in ~/.config/booping/config.yaml or the Project Vault's config.yaml, later levels winning. Query specs declared at any config path run with booping query --config or the | query filter; template trees are written with booping create, skipping an existing target unless --force. booping create and booping frontmatter-update print every write as a unified diff, silent when a file is unchanged. Argv macros under core.macros carry a command: and a cwd: repo|vault, and can be stubbed for reproducible renders. booping config-get reads any merged value and booping marker-set sets marker keys; core.plans.glob and core.research_agent are ordinary overridable keys.","claude-booping:story/0004":"As an advanced user, I want to write a rule that reaches only the surfaces it names, so that a project's rule shapes exactly the step it is about and nothing else. Scenarios The user writes a rule record in the project specs tier with status: accepted, a targets: list naming, for example, one step of a playbook, and a brief:. The user runs that playbook; its render carries a ## Rules section with the rule's brief and a link to the record. The targeted step's body carries the rule too; steps, agents and skills the rule does not name never see it. Edge cases A rule with no targets: injects nowhere. A rule that is not status: accepted is not injected. A rule meant for an external agent cannot target it with agent:; it targets the step that delegates to that agent. Criteria Rules are status: accepted rule records in the core, global and project specs tiers, each carrying a targets: list and a brief:. A target names a playbook, a step, an agent or a skill ({playbook}, {playbook}/{step}, agent:{id}, skill:{name}). Matched rules are injected as brief plus link into a playbook render's ## Rules section, each step's body and internal agent and skill bodies; an untargeted rule injects nowhere.","claude-booping:story/0005":"As an advanced user, I want to author plan and review templates, so that groom and code-review shape their artefacts the way my project needs. Scenarios The user drops a plan template, with its name and description frontmatter, into the Project Vault's plan_templates/. At drafting, groom offers it beside the templates the plugin ships, and drafts the plan against the one chosen. The user drops a review template into the Project Vault's review_templates/, or into the global level's to use it in every project on the machine. A code-review run offers it and reviews against the one chosen. Edge cases A project template carries the same name as a shipped one: it overrides the shipped one. A review template exists at both the global and the project level under one name: the project's wins. Criteria Plan templates in the Project Vault's plan_templates/ are offered by groom at drafting. Review templates in the Project Vault's review_templates/ or at the global level are offered by code-review runs.","claude-booping:story/0006":"As an advanced user, I want to write my own playbooks with the same mechanics as the core ones, so that my own procedures run under /playbook like the shipped loop. Scenarios The user creates a playbook in the Project Vault's _playbooks/ (or at the global level) — playbook.md, an optional playbook.yaml, one directory per step with its prompt.md — by hand or through the playbook-authoring playbook. /playbook lists it beside the core playbooks and drives it with the same mechanics: the step graph, parallel waves, subgraphs and, when declared, its states: machine. The user opts its bodies into Jinja with jinja: true and reads its own config namespace from them. The user adds an eval suite beside a step and runs it to guard the step's contract. Edge cases The user's playbook takes a name already used at another level: names must be unique across levels, so the clash blocks the run. A step directory holds no prompt.md: it is no step, and is skipped with a warning. Steps in one parallel wave must all be detached. Criteria A playbook at the global level or in the Project Vault's _playbooks/ — playbook.md, optional playbook.yaml, one directory per step with prompt.md — is discovered, names unique across levels. It declares a step graph with parallel waves and subgraphs, opt-in states: machines, and its own config namespace by the core placement rule. Bodies can be Jinja templates over the full context (jinja: true), resolved most-specific-first, and a step can hold per-model prompt variants. A step can carry its own eval suite (tests.yaml, promptfooconfig.yaml, fixtures). The playbook-authoring playbook creates a new playbook: brief, decomposition, manifest, then per step spec, fixtures, prompt and eval suite driven to green.","claude-booping:story/0007":"As an advanced user, I want to plug my own agents into booping's agent slots, so that runs use the agents I already have instead of only the shipped ones. Scenarios The user has an agent of their own in ~/.claude/agents/. The user points a playbook's agent slot at it in config, at the global or project level. Runs of that playbook delegate the slot's work to the user's agent. To shape how the agent works in this project, the user writes a rule targeting the step that delegates to it. Edge cases An agent: target names an external agent: it reaches nothing, since agent: targets reach booping's own agents only. Out of scope Extension files beside an agent: shaping is a targeted rule. Criteria A playbook's agent slot points at an external ~/.claude/agents/ agent via config. Any agent or skill is shaped per project by a targeted rule, not an extension file; agent: targets reach booping's own agents only, so an external agent is shaped by a rule on the step that delegates to it.","claude-booping:story/0008":"As a contributor, I want every skill and agent body rendered live at load time from one template source, so that an edit takes effect without a build step and a render can be reproduced anywhere. Scenarios A contributor edits a template under src/templates/. The next time Claude Code loads the skill or agent, its hand-authored thin shell runs booping render on that template, and the body renders with the full project context — config merge, vault, rules, playbooks — so the edit takes effect with no build step. To inspect a body without loading it, the contributor renders it by hand with booping render or booping render-playbook, and dumps the assembled context with booping debug-context when the output is unexpected. For a reproducible render, the contributor pins config values with --set, the timestamped macros with stubs, and the Project Vault with --project, and gets a byte-identical result. Edge cases A macro exits non-zero: the render is an error. The session macro runs outside a Claude Code session: it prints an empty string, so the render still succeeds. A render pinned with --project: discovery and the config merge see only the core and project levels, never the global one. Criteria Skill and agent bodies render in one runtime stage from src/templates/ with full project context; the committed skills/ and agents/ shells are hand-authored. booping render and booping render-playbook render any template or playbook by hand. --set dotted.key=value overrides one config value per render and timestamped values can be pinned, for byte-identical renders. --project pins a render to an explicit Project Vault, narrowing discovery and the config merge to the core and project levels. booping debug-context dumps the assembled render context as YAML.","claude-booping:story/0009":"As a contributor, I want one command to run every check a change must pass, so that a broken behaviour fails before it is committed. Scenarios A contributor finishes a change and runs just ci before committing. It runs lint, type-check and pytest, then diffs the committed playbook reports against a fresh hermetic render, runs the mdcheck structural rules over the rendered reports, checks the specs tiers and runs the txtar e2e corpora, in that order. Any failing check fails the run, and the contributor fixes the change before committing. When a playbook edit changes a report on purpose, the contributor reads the just snapshots diff and accepts the new baseline with just snapshots-accept. Edge cases A report drifts: just snapshots only shows the diff and writes nothing; just snapshots-accept is the only writer of a committed report. mdcheck is not installed: the checks that need it cannot run until the binary is installed. A -k selection matches cases in one e2e corpus only: the other collects nothing, which is not a failure. Criteria just ci runs lint, type-check and pytest (ruff, basedpyright, pytest) over the Python code. just snapshots diffs the committed playbook reports against a fresh hermetic render; just snapshots-accept accepts a new baseline. mdcheck structural rules run over every rendered report. Every booping subcommand's behaviour is a txtar end-to-end contract corpus run by just e2e.","claude-booping:story/0010":"As a contributor, I want the contract-case format and its sandboxed runner as a standalone pytest plugin, so that the e2e corpus depends on it like any other package. Scenarios A contributor adds a contract case for a booping subcommand as a txtar file: the fixtures, the command, and its expected exit code, stdout and stderr. just e2e runs the case through the plugin's sandboxed runner and compares the actual output with the expected one. When a behaviour changes on purpose, the contributor rebaselines the selected cases with --txtar-update. Edge cases --txtar-update runs the cases serially; a normal run runs them in parallel. Out of scope The case format's specification: it is owned by the plugin, not by booping. Criteria Contract cases are written in the txtar case format the plugin defines and run by its sandboxed runner. It is a PyPI dependency; booping's own e2e suite is its first consumer.","claude-booping:story/0011":"As a contributor, I want an eval suite beside each step, so that a prompt change that breaks the step's contract is caught. Scenarios A contributor changes a step's prompt. The contributor runs the step's deterministic tier with just smoke <playbook>/<step>, then its llm-rubric tier with just regress <playbook>/<step>, on subscription auth. The run posts a sticky, advisory comment on the pull request with the result. just suites lists the suites when the contributor looks for the one beside a step. Edge cases A failing suite blocks nothing: there is no commit status and no merge gate. The contributor does not want a PR comment: EVAL_PR=0 opts out. Out of scope Running the suites in CI. Criteria A suite's deterministic tier runs with just smoke and its llm-rubric tier with just regress. just suites lists every suite beside its step. Each run posts a sticky, advisory PR comment; there is no commit status and no merge gate, and the suites never run in CI.","claude-booping:story/0012":"As a contributor, I want to ship a vault migration with the plugin, so that a change to the vault shape reaches every existing Project Vault. Scenarios A contributor changes the vault shape and declares a migration at migrations/NNN_<slug>/migration.md, its id frontmatter the authority. The contributor authors it against the shipped primitives: query specs over core files, numeric --where ordering, the vault's applied watermark in templates, and marker writes. A user updates the plugin; every render surface sees the vault's watermark below the new migration and gates on it. The migration is applied and the watermark advances, and the render surfaces stop gating. Edge cases With core.auto_migrate on, the default, the notice orders the agent to run the migration unattended and resume; with it off, the run stops for the user. Criteria A migration is declared at migrations/NNN_<slug>/migration.md, its id frontmatter authoritative. Every render surface is gated on the Project Vault's applied watermark until the migration lands. A migration is authored against the primitives: numeric --where ordering, query specs over core files (root: core), {{ booping.latest_migration }} in templates, and marker writes via booping marker-set.","claude-booping:story/0013":"As a contributor, I want the public reference built strictly and published on every push to master, so that the docs site tracks what is shipped. Scenarios A contributor edits a page under documentation/ and previews it live with just docs-serve. The contributor builds the site with just docs, which builds it strictly. The change is pushed to master, and the site is published to gh-pages. Edge cases [clarify] What the strict build rejects is not named in the sources. Out of scope The plugin-internal fragments under docs/: they are lazy-loaded by skills, not published. Criteria just docs builds the site strictly and just docs-serve previews it live. documentation/ publishes to gh-pages on push to master.","claude-booping:story/0014":"As a user, I want one playbook that takes my repo from any state to a working booping project, so that I can start the loop without wiring anything by hand. Scenarios The user runs /playbook setup in a repo with nothing set up yet. The playbook settles the machine level first: it resolves the home dir and writes the machine-level config. It then settles the project level: it asks for the vault location — the default under the home dir or a repo-local directory — creates the Project Vault from the config-declared tree, and writes the .booping marker that attaches the repo. The user runs /playbook in the same repo and sees the shipped playbooks listed with no STOP notice, ready for a first groom. Re-running: the user runs /playbook setup again on a wired-up project; every phase is detected as satisfied and the run reports the state instead of changing it. Edge cases A repo-local vault: the marker carries vault_path: and the vault is symlinked into the home dir for Obsidian. A fresh vault starts at the highest shipped migration id, so no migration notice appears on the first render. sprints.md already exists: it is never regenerated, the seeded view stays the user's. Criteria Resolves and writes machine-level config with a working home_dir. Creates the Project Vault from the config-declared tree, including sprints.md as a live Obsidian Bases view seeded once, never regenerated. Writes the marker to attach the repo (vault_path: for a repo-local vault) and symlinks a repo-local vault into the home dir for Obsidian. Seeds a fresh Project Vault at the highest shipped migration id, so no migration is pending. Detects phases already satisfied and skips them, so a re-run reports state instead of changing it.","claude-booping:story/0015":"As a user, I want to bring my existing Project Vault current with a new plugin version, so that an upgrade does not leave my vault in a shape the playbooks no longer read. Scenarios The user updates the plugin; the next render sees the marker's latest_migration watermark behind the shipped migrations and prints a notice in place of its output. The user runs /playbook migrate; it surveys the pending migrations against the watermark and lists them. The user approves once, up front; the playbook applies each pending migration in id order, one Project Vault commit per migration, advancing the watermark after each. The renders stop gating and the user's original request goes on. Unattended: with core.auto_migrate on, the default, the notice orders the agent to run /playbook migrate itself and then resume the original request. Edge cases A migration fails: the run halts, naming the id, the failure and a remedy the user can apply by hand. An older vault still holds _booping/ skill and agent extension files: they become targeted rules and the originals are removed. core.auto_migrate is off: the notice stops the run and tells the user to run /playbook migrate; nothing else renders until the vault catches up. Criteria Every render surface warns when the marker's latest_migration watermark is behind. Surveys the pending plugin-shipped migrations against the watermark. Applies each pending migration in id order on one up-front approval, one Project Vault commit per migration. Converts an older vault's _booping/ skill and agent extension files into targeted rules, removing the originals. Halts on a failing migration, naming the id, the failure and a hand-applicable remedy.","claude-booping:story/0016":"As a user, I want a feature, bug or refactor shaped into a specified, estimated plan I approved, so that develop can carry it out without coming back to me. Scenarios The user runs /playbook groom with a request; intake clarifies it, settles the scope and creates the plan directory with its briefing and a commit: at repo HEAD. draft-plan researches the code the request touches, handing bulk reads to the research agent, and drafts the whole plan into draft.md against the matching plan template. booping plan split cuts the draft into index.md and one milestone file per milestone; mdcheck checks the milestones against the contract and the run fixes what it flags. present shows the approach, milestones, SP totals and the check; the user approves and the plan lands at ready-for-dev. Change request: at present the user asks for a change touching the plan itself; the run goes back to drafting, edits draft.md and splits again. Edge cases A call only the user can make, in an autonomous run: the question is written to the plan's clarifications.md and the run parks at awaiting-clarification. A draft the split cannot cut: it goes to the split fallback agent, which rewrites it into the format, and the run splits again. A task estimated at the redecompose threshold or above: the run cannot leave drafting until it is split. The user corrects the run: the correction is logged verbatim under corrections/. Criteria Clarifies the request at intake before any drafting. Drafts the whole plan in one pass: draft-plan researches the code in-session, delegating bulk reads to the researcher, then drafts against the vault's plan template (adr/0030). Creates plans/{slug}/index.md and one file per milestone under milestones/ with booping plan split from the one-file draft (adr/0031). Drafts milestones, tasks and SP estimates and checks the milestone contract with mdcheck (adr/0038); no separate review step (adr/0035). Stops at a single approval gate near the end, landing the plan at ready-for-dev.","claude-booping:story/0017":"As a user, I want a groomed plan executed one milestone group at a time by a worker agent, so that the plan is carried to the end while the runner checks every milestone. Scenarios The user runs /playbook develop, naming a plan or picking one from the table of plans ready for development; intake checks the plan against the repo's current shape. provision proposes the sprint branch and waits for the user's confirmation before touching git, then settles the milestone groups. For each group the runner briefs the worker with the milestone files by path; the worker implements them, runs each milestone's Verify and leaves the work uncommitted. The runner validates the working tree against the DoD, commits the milestone, ticks its checkboxes and takes its transition. verify runs the project's gates and the plan's Final Verification; wrap-up closes the plan at done and hands off to /playbook retro. Resuming: the user invokes develop on a plan already in-progress; the run picks up at the first milestone not done. Edge cases A tree short of the DoD or a red Verify: the runner writes feedback.md beside the milestone and briefs a fresh worker with it; after two attempts the run asks the user to approve the abort and the plan ends at fail. A sprint branch that already exists: it is reused rather than recreated. The user corrects the runner mid-sprint: the correction is logged verbatim under corrections/. Criteria Resolves the plan from the invocation, or offers a table of plans ready for development. Hands the configured worker one briefing per SP group of milestones, the milestone files by path as the whole work contract (adr/0022); the runner never writes application code. The worker runs its milestone's Verify and leaves the work uncommitted; the runner validates it against the DoD and makes the repo commit. Works one milestone group at a time on the sprint branch, never two workers at once. Verifies the definition of done and the project guardrails, closes the plan at done or fail, and hands off to /playbook retro.","claude-booping:story/0018":"As a user, I want a finished sprint mined into a retrospective, so that what went wrong is found by root cause and can be learnt from. Scenarios The user runs /playbook retro; intake validates the plan is in the retro queue and offers the other queued plans to include, postpone or skip. prepare reads the open corrections, marks each handled, and mines the session logs and the sprint diff, keeping the mined list back. gather-feedback asks the user's raw take first, one question at a time, then walks the mined items for accept, dismiss or the user's own wording. research-issues root-causes each accepted issue; synthesize drafts the retrospective and shows a summary. save writes retrospectives/{slug}.md and stamps retro: on every covered plan; the retrospective waits for learn. Edge cases A plan not worth a retrospective: the user picks skip and the plan gets retro: skipped, out of the queue for good, its status still done. Several queued plans: one retrospective covers them all, each plan pointing back through its own retro: key. Criteria Queues every done plan not yet covered (retro: null). Mines session logs and the sprint diff for what actually happened. Reads the open corrections (corrections/, adr/0026) first and marks what it handled. Gathers the user's raw feedback and triages issues by root cause. Saves retrospectives/{slug}.md and stamps each covered plan's retro: key, the plan staying at done.","claude-booping:story/0019":"As a user, I want retrospective findings turned into durable behaviour changes I confirmed, so that the next runs do not repeat the same mistakes. Scenarios The user runs /playbook learn on a retrospective at awaiting-learning. The run decomposes the retrospective into atomic candidates, each routed to one destination, and sweeps them against the rules of the three specs tiers and the repo's CLAUDE.md: update, create, covered or conflict. It reads the playbook table of contents and the targets of each touched playbook, and presents one review table, each rule row carrying its exact targets:. The user accepts all, rejects rows by number or adds a row; the run writes the accepted items and closes the retrospective at done. Edge cases The project has no specs tier yet: the run creates it before writing the first rule. A finding a core or global rule already holds: shown as covered, never written. A finding that contradicts an existing rule: flagged a conflict for the user to resolve in the review table. A user row carrying two rules: split into two before writing. Criteria Extracts atomic candidates from the retrospective and sweeps them against existing rules. Shows the full playbook table of contents and fetches the targets of each touched playbook. Confirms a review table with the user before anything is written, each row carrying its exact targets:. Routes each accepted item to exactly one destination: a rule record in the project specs tier or the repo's CLAUDE.md. Closes the covered retrospective at done.","claude-booping:story/0020":"As a user, I want each plan's real effort measured from the session logs, so that I can see what a sprint cost in time, tokens and models. Scenarios The user grooms and develops a plan; each transition records the session ids that worked on it in the plan's frontmatter. Develop closes the plan; booping session-stats mines those session logs and the plan gets its metrics_* keys: active minutes, models and token counts. The user opens sprints.md and sees each sprint's cost in its columns, without opening the plans. The user runs booping session-stats directly to see the same numbers broken down per session. Edge cases Time spent waiting on the user — a question unanswered, a tool call rejected — is not counted as active. One API message logged as several lines, one per content block: its tokens are counted once. A plan that ends at fail is stamped too; no other edge stamps the totals. Out of scope Sub-agent transcripts: only the main thread is measured. Criteria booping session-stats computes a plan's active work minutes, discounting time spent waiting on a human. Each session's tokens (input, output, cache-creation, cache-read) are accounted beside the model ids that ran the work. Groom and develop stamp their session ids on the plan; stamp-active-time stamps the totals as metrics_* plan frontmatter on develop's done and fail edges only. Active minutes and models show as sprints.md and query columns.","claude-booping:story/0021":"As a user, I want a confirmed scope reviewed end to end with my verdict before any fix, so that only the fixes I approve land. Scenarios The user runs /playbook code-review; scope offers every done plan with its review history, the latest commits, or a named target, and the user confirms one. The run opens the review file under codereviews/ and offers the review templates. review returns severity-classified findings from a detached pass; present writes them and asks the user's verdict on each. resolve applies the approved fixes, records every finding as applied, delegated or dropped, appends the review to the plan's code_reviews: and commits the vault. Re-review: the user reviews the same plan again after fixes; it is a new, separately recorded run and the plan stays in the queue. Edge cases An ad-hoc scope with no plan: the review carries plan: null and closing appends nothing to any plan. A stopped review: it resumes from the review file's own status. The user overrules a finding's severity or rejects a fix: the correction is logged verbatim under corrections/. Criteria Settles the scope: a delivered plan's diff, the latest commits, or a named ad-hoc target. Opens codereviews/{dir}/{ts}.md, carrying the reviewed plan or plan: null, and offers the vault's and global review templates. Returns severity-classified findings from a detached review pass. Collects the human verdict and applies only the approved fixes. Appends the closed review to the plan's code_reviews: history, never touching plan status.","claude-booping:story/0022":"As an advanced user, I want my runs mirrored onto my issue tracker, so that the tracker shows where a plan stands without me copying it by hand. Scenarios The user names a tracker provider in core.tracker.driver and the environment variable holding its key in api_key_env. A groom run moves the plan along its edges; each edge's tracker-sync hook pushes the plan's status to the tracker issue through the configured status map. Groom parks on a question; tracker-comment posts clarifications.md to the issue verbatim. The user checks the issue on the tracker and sees where the plan stands. Without a tracker: the shipped cli provider prints receipts and writes nothing, so a project with no tracker runs as before. Edge cases The tracker fails during a hook: the hook only warns, the transition completes, and booping-tracker sync --artifact … by hand reconciles. A call with --dry-run: no provider is reached. An unknown driver name: a hard error. Criteria bin/booping-tracker is the only path from playbooks and hook scripts to a tracker, with the verbs states, show, comment, issue-create, issue-update, relate and sync; --dry-run reaches no provider. The provider is named by core.tracker.driver (--driver per call); the one shipped provider, cli, prints receipts and writes nothing, so a project without a tracker behaves as before, and a real tracker slots in as another provider. Groom's edges run tracker-sync to push the plan's status through the configured status map, and tracker-comment to post clarifications.md; sync is idempotent. A tracker failure in a hook only warns, so a transition never aborts with the vault advanced; booping-tracker sync --artifact … by hand reconciles. Secrets are never in config: api_key_env names the environment variable holding the key.","claude-booping:story/0023":"As a user, I want one manager to talk to that turns what I say into tracked work and carries it to the end, so that no request, question or finished run gets lost between tracks. Scenarios The user opens /playbook project-manager; it posts booping status and names what to act on first: finished or lost runs, items waiting on the user, plans ready for develop, plans without a review, open items. The user writes a message with several requests; the manager files each as an item in the user's words and lists the items it created. A planned item runs groom, then booping run develop and booping run code-review as recorded runs; the next turn surfaces each finished run, and the branch is merged locally on the user's yes. A question the manager cannot settle goes into the item's body and the item moves to clarify; the user answers and the item returns to open. Tiny fixes: in an autonomous project, small items are sized and packed into one worker briefing, committed once per item and closed without grooming. Edge cases An instruction for this session, an answer or a gate confirmation: no item is filed. A develop run that fails: reported with its log, never retried silently. A second develop or code-review run on the same checkout: queued until the running one ends. An autonomous session: the manager settles the plan approval and the merge itself and logs each as an agent decision. Out of scope Pushing, opening a pull request or deploying: only when a project rule says how. Criteria Every user message is split into items as it arrives, asides included, each keeping the user's words verbatim (adr/0025). \"What is going on\" is computed by code from items, plans, runs and unreviewed records — no stored handoff that can go stale. A question lives in its item's body, the item parked in clarify; chat only points to the item. Every launched run is recorded with its item, and a finished run surfaces on the next turn. In an autonomous project, small items are batched by size and skip grooming.","claude-booping:story/0024":"As the owner, I want the system to review my specs and bring me only what needs my decision, so that the specs stay true without me rereading them. Scenarios The owner asks the specs playbook for a review; it reads every record type in every tier the project reaches and puts each record to its type test. A record that is plainly stale — it only needs to say what is already decided or true — is fixed and the fix is reported. Each decision that is the owner's is filed as a clarify item; in the dialogue the system asks them one at a time in prose, with compact context, its own proposal and a counter. Before the owner accepts anything, the system lists what it is about to take as decided. Restart: the session ends mid-queue; the next review picks the remaining questions up from the open items. Edge cases The review uncovers an incident: it is written up as a reference tagged postmortem — timeline, causes, remedies. A contradiction the records themselves settle: it becomes a conflict record; only one the owner alone can settle becomes an open item. Criteria Reads every record type in every tier the project reaches and puts each record to its type test (booping:rule/0034). Applies a fix that only makes a record say what is already decided or true, and reports it. Files every decision that is mine as a clarify item, so a restart picks the queue up from the open items; in the dialogue it asks one at a time in prose, with compact context, its own proposal and a counter (booping:rule/0032). Lists what it is about to take as decided before I accept anything (booping:rule/0033). An incident it uncovers is written up as a reference tagged postmortem: timeline, causes, remedies.","claude-booping:story/0025":"As an advanced user, I want to wire my own triggers, schedules, trackers and usage guards on top of booping, so that the core stays a framework and my setup stays mine (value/0017). Scenarios The user's scheduler, board event or messenger hook files the incoming work as an open item with booping create, whatever channel it came from. The same trigger launches a playbook run on it, for example booping run groom or booping run develop. The user shapes the core playbooks for their setup with project or global playbooks, hooks and rules carrying targets. The user's own guard decides when a run may start; booping runs it as any other run. Edge cases A setup with no integration at all: booping works as shipped, driven by hand. Out of scope A scheduler, a trigger, a tracker-specific integration or a usage gate in the core. Criteria A run starts from anything that can launch a playbook run: a scheduler, a board event, a messenger hook. Incoming work enters as an open item, whatever channel it came from. Project or global playbooks, hooks and rules with targets shape the core playbooks for the setup. The core ships no scheduler, no trigger, no tracker-specific integration and no usage gate.","claude-booping:story/0026":"As the owner, I want a decision made in the specs to become tracked work that the code follows, so that the code never runs ahead of a decision and nothing decided is forgotten. Scenarios The owner records an ADR whose Confirmation: line the code does not show yet; it becomes an open item running specs to code. The owner names the item in a groom request; groom links it to the new plan and the item moves to planned. Develop carries the plan out; when the plan ends at done, the item closes. A sync of the specs against the code finds code with no record behind it; that gap becomes an open item running code to specs. Edge cases The plan fails or is cancelled: its linked items return to open. A plan cancelled some other way leaves its items planned; reopening them by hand puts them back. Criteria A decision that needs code becomes an open item running specs to code: from an ADR's confirmation line, or from a sync of the specs against the code. Groom links the item to the plan it grooms, and develop carries that plan out. The item closes when its plan is done and reopens when the plan fails or is cancelled. Code that ran ahead of the specs becomes an open item running code to specs.","claude-booping:value/0001":"The system gets better from its own runs, but only through the user and when the user wants it: a lesson becomes a rule after the user takes it, not after every run. In practice Retro and learn run when the user asks; what they propose becomes a rule only with the user's consent. Trade-off Mistakes may repeat until the user runs a retro.","claude-booping:value/0002":"A step gets only what it needs. What the model already knows does not clutter the context; skills and steps carry only the information that matters — ten lines, not two hundred, when ten work. In practice Detail is linked and read on demand, never inlined in case it helps; a rule reaches a step as a short brief with a link. Trade-off More files and indirection; a step may miss something it was not handed.","claude-booping:value/0003":"Each fact, decision and piece of structured data lives in one place and is referenced or rendered from there; a decision is kept together with its reason. In practice Config and records are the source; prose never restates them. Trade-off A render pipeline and tooling to maintain.","claude-booping:value/0004":"Plans, runs and their outcomes are kept across every project, so the history can be analysed: practices taken over from one project to another, benchmarks, quality comparison, problem analysis. In practice Plans, retros and reviews stay in the vault after they close; their shape stays stable enough to compare across projects and time. Trade-off Storage and a stable artefact shape that is harder to change.","claude-booping:value/0005":"The core knows nothing about a stack or a project; specifics live in the global and the project tiers. In practice Skills, playbooks and the specs skeleton stay generic and leave enough room to extend booping in userland: the global and the project tiers add and override — their own plan templates, playbooks, rules. Trade-off [clarify] proposed: the core ships no stack-specific defaults; whoever wants them writes them in their own tier.","claude-booping:value/0006":"Booping is a framework for playbooks. A user extends it by digitising their own processes — writing user stories, designing an architecture, any procedure — with the tools booping gives: config, templates, primitives, the CLI, playbooks and evals for their steps. (owner, 2026-10-17) In practice A project's config extends booping's logic: user playbooks, scripts and templates read their own config keys, and the core mechanics (state machines, hooks, queries, template trees, tiers) carry them. Trade-off no schema gate on config: a mistyped key is not caught at load.","claude-booping:value/0007":"The agent carries a plan through to the end on its own unless force majeure stops it. Problems are found while planning, discussed with the user in that session, and not solved ad hoc during the run. In practice Grooming hunts for open questions and settles them with the user before ready-for-dev; develop does not stop to ask what grooming should have asked. Trade-off Longer planning sessions.","claude-booping:value/0008":"Every step where a human takes part is convenient and effective for that human. The cheap, compact artefact comes first and is checked before the expensive detail is built on it, so a review never has to go several steps back because the first lines already show the wrong thing was made. In practice User stories start from a feature index — a compact table with capacity and breakdown the human can read, reorder, fix and decompose — and only then a story per feature; grooming and planning work the same way. Trade-off More review checkpoints in a run.","claude-booping:value/0009":"The human can review the system deeply, or give the agent more freedom to act inside the values and boundaries. Either way the human can take control back easily and review the values and boundaries when things go the wrong way. In practice What the agent did without review stays visible (reviewed_at or its equivalent), so the human can find it and analyse it later. Trade-off The agent marks and records instead of silently deciding, which costs it time.","claude-booping:value/0010":"We know the system works as it should and can guarantee it high enough to call it reliable. Tests, snapshots, evals and checks are the tools, not the value. In practice A behaviour that matters is held by a check that would fail if it broke. Trade-off The cost of building and keeping the test infrastructure.","claude-booping:value/0011":"Everything is plain files, easy to look at from one place — for example Obsidian; symlinks back to the project repos keep it together. In practice Markdown and YAML in git; project specs are reachable from the user's home through symlinks. Trade-off No database queries and no concurrent writes.","claude-booping:value/0012":"Booping is a work tool for professionals, so the quality of the result matters. It spends tokens rationally, without token-maxing, and keeps a balance between simplicity and the quality of the result. In practice Step prompts are evaluated and narrowed while the evals show the result still holds; a step's prompt is tuned to a specific model and copied and re-tuned for another, with evals working as unit tests for the steps; what a sub-agent already knows when asked about a prompt detail does not belong in the prompt; lessons are written as compact rules with examples, not as a raw retro. Trade-off A run sometimes costs more than the cheapest one that would pass.","claude-booping:value/0013":"The system is clear to the user and leads them to the goal by the hand, while letting a professional step aside and get there their own way. In practice After a chat about a task and a grooming run, the system says how to call develop; given an under-groomed plan, it says so and what to do next. Trade-off Hints the user may not need.","claude-booping:value/0014":"Booping is a work tool for professionals with a strong technical background. How comfortable it is for people without that background is not what we optimise for — they are not our audience. In practice \"Guides to the goal\" guides a professional; it does not explain the basics of git, the terminal, tests or planning.","claude-booping:value/0015":"What code reads — a guard, a status report, another command, a slice filled into a prompt at render time — and every fact outside the model's view — the time, the session id, a process, a transcript, many files at once — is produced by code, so it is the same on every model and every run. What only a model or a human reads — instructions, a mode of behaviour, findings, a note someone reviews — stays prose, with a fixed format when its shape matters. Code over that format is an optimisation, taken when a reader in code appears; a reader that only the proposed design would add does not count. In practice Playbooks render by code, rules and config values are injected by code, state transitions and their hooks run as code, and a transition's printed report is trusted without re-reading. Whether a run is autonomous is prose (adr/0036); journal entries are vault files that rendered prompts slice through query (adr/0037). Trade-off Prose drifts from its format where no code checks it; a format fixed in docs/ keeps the later move to code cheap.","claude-booping:value/0016":"Specs are updated in the course of the work, not in a separate pass the user has to remember, and they do not go stale. They are applied wherever they add quality to a result and knowledge of the decisions already made (owner, 2026-10-16). In practice A decision made in a groom, a scenario the owner describes in passing, a departure a sprint makes from an accepted ADR — each reaches the specs while the work happens, as a draft the owner confirms. A playbook that decides looks at what the specs already settle instead of deciding again. Trade-off Some cost on every run that touches the specs, and drafts the owner has to confirm.","claude-booping:value/0017":"Booping does not solve how development is launched: triggers from a task moving on a board, a Jira or Multica integration, an event in a messenger such as ntfy, a schedule or a nightly run are userland. The same holds for the subscription's usage limits: booping never stops a user from spending them, and a user who wants a guard builds it. At most booping offers a surface to integrate through, such as rules and hooks (owner, 2026-10-17). In practice The core ships no scheduler, no trigger, no tracker-specific integration and no usage gate; a user builds them on top of booping run, booping items add and playbook hooks, at the global or the project tier.","claude-booping:value/0018":"On top of the framework (value/0006) booping ships a core set of playbooks for development — groom, develop, code-review, project-manager and self-learning (value/0001). Those playbooks are also booping's own dogfooding (owner, 2026-10-17). In practice A core playbook uses only mechanics a user playbook gets too; what the core set needs and the framework lacks becomes a framework mechanic, not a private shortcut.","claude-booping:value/0019":"Booping decomposes work into plans and their sprints, so a project can be written along its roadmap, sprint by sprint; once work runs in sprints, a manager that carries it through grooming, development, review and merge follows naturally (owner, 2026-10-18). In practice The project-manager playbook files every request as an item and works the items through groom, develop and code-review in the project's autonomy mode. Trade-off Even a small request passes through the item store before it is worked."};
